/* Pharmacology 10: Antibacterials */
Z("pharm10",0,"ph",["Antimicrobials are classified by chemical structure, mechanism of action, spectrum and type of action (bacteriostatic or bactericidal); combinations are used to widen cover, achieve synergy, prevent resistance or reduce toxicity.","Each antibiotic attacks a different part of the bacterial 'factory' — the wall, the membrane, the protein machine, the DNA copier or the folate kitchen.",["By mechanism: cell wall synthesis inhibitors (β-lactams, vancomycin, bacitracin, fosfomycin, cycloserine); cell membrane (polymyxins, daptomycin, amphotericin, azoles); protein synthesis — 30S (aminoglycosides, tetracyclines), 50S (macrolides, chloramphenicol, clindamycin, linezolid); nucleic acid (fluoroquinolones, rifampicin, metronidazole); antimetabolites (sulfonamides, trimethoprim, PAS, dapsone).","Bactericidal: β-lactams, aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, vancomycin, rifampicin, metronidazole, cotrimoxazole; bacteriostatic: tetracyclines, macrolides, chloramphenicol, clindamycin, sulfonamides, linezolid.","Combination uses: synergism (penicillin + gentamicin in enterococcal endocarditis; sulfamethoxazole + trimethoprim; β-lactam + β-lactamase inhibitor), prevent resistance (TB, H. pylori, HIV), mixed infections, empirical therapy in severe sepsis/febrile neutropenia, reduce toxicity (amphotericin + flucytosine).","Disadvantages: antagonism (bacteriostatic + bactericidal), ↑ toxicity, superinfection, cost, false sense of security."],"Protein synthesis: 'Buy AT 30, CCEL at 50' — Aminoglycosides, Tetracyclines (30S); Chloramphenicol, Clindamycin, Erythromycin, Linezolid (50S)."],
"Antimicrobial agents (AMAs) are classified in several ways. (A) Chemical structure: sulfonamides and related (sulfadiazine, dapsone, PAS); diaminopyrimidines (trimethoprim, pyrimethamine); quinolones (nalidixic acid, ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin, moxifloxacin); β-lactams (penicillins, cephalosporins, carbapenems, monobactams); tetracyclines (doxycycline, minocycline, tigecycline); nitrobenzene derivative (chloramphenicol); aminoglycosides (streptomycin, gentamicin, amikacin); macrolides (erythromycin, azithromycin, clarithromycin); lincosamides (clindamycin); glycopeptides (vancomycin, teicoplanin); oxazolidinones (linezolid); polypeptides (polymyxin B, colistin, bacitracin); nitrofurans (nitrofurantoin); nitroimidazoles (metronidazole, tinidazole); nicotinic acid derivatives (isoniazid, pyrazinamide); polyenes (amphotericin B, nystatin); azoles (fluconazole, voriconazole); others (rifampicin, fosfomycin, daptomycin). (B) Mechanism of action: (1) inhibit cell wall synthesis — penicillins, cephalosporins, carbapenems, monobactams (transpeptidation/PBPs), vancomycin (binds D-Ala-D-Ala), bacitracin, cycloserine, fosfomycin; (2) damage cell membrane — polymyxins, colistin, daptomycin, polyene antifungals, azoles (ergosterol synthesis); (3) inhibit protein synthesis — 30S subunit: aminoglycosides (misreading of mRNA — bactericidal), tetracyclines (block aminoacyl-tRNA attachment); 50S subunit: chloramphenicol (peptidyl transferase), macrolides and clindamycin (translocation), linezolid (initiation complex), streptogramins; (4) inhibit nucleic acid synthesis/function — fluoroquinolones (DNA gyrase/topoisomerase IV), rifampicin (DNA-dependent RNA polymerase), metronidazole (DNA strand breakage), nitrofurantoin; (5) antimetabolites (folate synthesis) — sulfonamides (dihydropteroate synthase), trimethoprim and pyrimethamine (DHFR), PAS, dapsone; (6) interfere with DNA function/viral replication — acyclovir, zidovudine; (7) mycolic acid synthesis — isoniazid. (C) Type of organisms: antibacterial, antifungal, antiviral, antiprotozoal, anthelmintic. (D) Spectrum: narrow (penicillin G, streptomycin, erythromycin, vancomycin), extended (ampicillin), broad (tetracyclines, chloramphenicol, carbapenems, fluoroquinolones, third/fourth generation cephalosporins). (E) Type of action: bacteriostatic — sulfonamides, tetracyclines, chloramphenicol, macrolides, clindamycin, linezolid, ethambutol; bactericidal — penicillins, cephalosporins, aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, vancomycin, rifampicin, isoniazid, metronidazole, cotrimoxazole, polymyxins; this depends on drug concentration and organism. (F) Source: fungi (penicillin, griseofulvin), bacteria (polymyxin, bacitracin), actinomycetes (aminoglycosides, tetracyclines, chloramphenicol, macrolides). Combined use of antimicrobials — indications: (1) to achieve synergism — penicillin/ampicillin + gentamicin/streptomycin in enterococcal and viridans streptococcal endocarditis (cell wall damage enhances aminoglycoside entry); sulfamethoxazole + trimethoprim (sequential blockade); β-lactam + β-lactamase inhibitor (amoxicillin + clavulanate); carbenicillin/piperacillin + gentamicin in Pseudomonas; rifampicin + vancomycin in MRSA prosthetic infection; (2) to reduce severity or incidence of adverse effects — amphotericin B + flucytosine (cryptococcal meningitis; lower amphotericin dose); (3) to prevent emergence of resistance — tuberculosis (multidrug regimens), leprosy (MDT), H. pylori, HIV (HAART), malaria (ACT); (4) to broaden spectrum — mixed infections (peritonitis, aspiration pneumonia, diabetic foot, pelvic infections — e.g., ceftriaxone + metronidazole), empirical therapy in serious infections when the organism is unknown (septicaemia, febrile neutropenia, meningitis), topical combinations (polymyxin + neomycin + bacitracin). Disadvantages: antagonism (a bacteriostatic drug may antagonise a bactericidal drug that acts on dividing cells, e.g., tetracycline + penicillin in pneumococcal meningitis), increased adverse effects and drug interactions, superinfection with resistant organisms, higher cost, encouragement of irrational prescribing (fixed-dose combinations), inadequate dose of individual components, and a false sense of security delaying diagnosis. Rational use: culture and sensitivity, appropriate choice based on likely organism, site, host factors (age, pregnancy, renal/hepatic function, allergy, immune status), pharmacokinetics/pharmacodynamics (time-dependent vs concentration-dependent killing), de-escalation and antimicrobial stewardship to limit resistance.",
[["Classification by mechanism",[["Mechanism","Drugs"],["Cell wall synthesis","Penicillins, cephalosporins, carbapenems, monobactams, vancomycin, bacitracin, fosfomycin, cycloserine"],["Cell membrane","Polymyxin B, colistin, daptomycin, amphotericin B, azoles"],["Protein synthesis 30S","Aminoglycosides, tetracyclines"],["Protein synthesis 50S","Chloramphenicol, macrolides, clindamycin, linezolid"],["Nucleic acid","Fluoroquinolones, rifampicin, metronidazole, nitrofurantoin"],["Folate antimetabolites","Sulfonamides, trimethoprim, pyrimethamine, dapsone, PAS"],["Mycolic acid","Isoniazid, ethionamide"]]],
["Bacteriostatic vs bactericidal",[["Bacteriostatic","Bactericidal"],["Tetracyclines","Penicillins, cephalosporins"],["Chloramphenicol","Aminoglycosides"],["Macrolides, clindamycin","Fluoroquinolones"],["Sulfonamides, trimethoprim (alone)","Cotrimoxazole"],["Linezolid, ethambutol","Vancomycin, rifampicin, isoniazid, metronidazole"]]],
["Indications for combination",[["Purpose","Example"],["Synergism","Penicillin + gentamicin (enterococcal endocarditis); SMX + TMP"],["Prevent resistance","TB, leprosy, H. pylori, HIV, malaria (ACT)"],["Broaden spectrum","Mixed infections; empirical sepsis therapy"],["Reduce toxicity","Amphotericin B + flucytosine"]]],
["Disadvantages of combination",["Antagonism (static + cidal)","Increased toxicity and interactions","Superinfection","Cost","Irrational fixed-dose combinations"]]],
{i:"Understanding antimicrobial classification by mechanism and type of action guides rational selection and combination therapy.",f:"Bacterial targets:\n Cell wall ← β-lactams, vancomycin\n Membrane ← polymyxins, daptomycin\n Ribosome 30S ← aminoglycosides, tetracyclines; 50S ← macrolides, chloramphenicol, clindamycin, linezolid\n DNA/RNA ← quinolones, rifampicin, metronidazole\n Folate ← sulfonamides + trimethoprim",c:"Antimicrobials are best remembered by their target; combinations are justified for synergy, resistance prevention, broad empirical cover and reduced toxicity, but carry risks of antagonism and toxicity and must be used rationally."});

Z("pharm10",1,"ph",["Sulfonamides are synthetic bacteriostatic antimicrobials that block bacterial folic acid synthesis by competing with PABA; today they are mainly used in combination (cotrimoxazole) and for special indications.","Bacteria must build their own folate from PABA; sulfonamides are fake PABA that jam the assembly line.",["MOA: structural analogue of PABA → competitively inhibits dihydropteroate synthase → ↓ folate → bacteriostatic; humans use preformed folate (selective toxicity).","Classification: short-acting (sulfadiazine, sulfisoxazole), intermediate (sulfamethoxazole), long-acting (sulfadoxine — with pyrimethamine), topical (silver sulfadiazine, mafenide — burns; sulfacetamide — eye), poorly absorbed (sulfasalazine — IBD).","Uses: cotrimoxazole (UTI, PCP), sulfadoxine–pyrimethamine (malaria with artesunate), sulfadiazine + pyrimethamine (toxoplasmosis), silver sulfadiazine (burns), sulfacetamide eye drops, nocardiosis.","ADR: crystalluria (drink water, alkalinise urine), hypersensitivity (rash, SJS/TEN, fever), haemolysis in G6PD deficiency, kernicterus in neonates (displace bilirubin — avoid late pregnancy/newborn), bone marrow suppression, hepatitis; interactions — displace warfarin, sulfonylureas, methotrexate."],"Sulfa in newborn → kernicterus; in G6PD → haemolysis."],
"Sulfonamides were the first effective systemic antibacterial agents (Domagk, prontosil, 1935). Chemistry: derivatives of sulfanilamide (para-aminobenzene sulfonamide), structurally similar to para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA). Mechanism: bacteria synthesise folic acid from PABA, pteridine and glutamate; sulfonamides competitively inhibit dihydropteroate synthase (and may be incorporated forming non-functional analogues), reducing dihydrofolate and thus tetrahydrofolate needed for purine, thymidine and amino acid synthesis — bacteriostatic. Human cells are unaffected because they utilise preformed dietary folate. Action is antagonised by PABA and its sources (procaine, pus, tissue debris — ineffective in presence of pus). Spectrum: originally broad — Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria (streptococci, pneumococci, gonococci, meningococci, E. coli, Shigella, H. ducreyi), Nocardia, Chlamydia trachomatis, Toxoplasma, Plasmodium, Pneumocystis; widespread resistance now (altered dihydropteroate synthase, increased PABA production, reduced permeability; plasmid-mediated). Classification: (1) short-acting (t½ 4–8 h) — sulfadiazine, sulfisoxazole (sulfafurazole); (2) intermediate (8–12 h) — sulfamethoxazole; (3) long-acting (> 50 h; ~7 days) — sulfadoxine, sulfamethopyrazine; (4) special purpose — sulfacetamide sodium (topical ocular), mafenide, silver sulfadiazine (topical burns), sulfasalazine (ulcerative colitis, rheumatoid arthritis). Pharmacokinetics: rapid oral absorption (except sulfasalazine), widely distributed including CSF (sulfadiazine), cross placenta; variable protein binding; acetylated in liver (acetylated derivatives less soluble — crystalluria); renal excretion. Uses (now limited because of resistance and toxicity): (1) combination with trimethoprim — cotrimoxazole (UTI, Pneumocystis, nocardiosis, toxoplasmosis); (2) sulfadoxine + pyrimethamine with artesunate (ACT for falciparum malaria in India; intermittent preventive therapy in pregnancy elsewhere); (3) sulfadiazine + pyrimethamine for toxoplasmosis (with folinic acid); (4) topical — silver sulfadiazine 1% cream and mafenide for burns (prevent infection, Pseudomonas), sulfacetamide 10–30% eye drops for trachoma and conjunctivitis; (5) sulfasalazine for ulcerative colitis and rheumatoid arthritis; (6) nocardiosis; (7) chancroid, lymphogranuloma venereum (historical). Adverse effects: (1) crystalluria — precipitation in acidic urine causing haematuria, renal colic, obstruction (avoid with adequate fluids and alkalinisation); (2) hypersensitivity — rashes, urticaria, fever, photosensitivity, exfoliative dermatitis, Stevens–Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis (especially long-acting), serum sickness, vasculitis, polyarteritis; (3) haematological — haemolytic anaemia in G6PD deficiency, agranulocytosis, thrombocytopenia, aplastic anaemia, megaloblastic anaemia; (4) kernicterus in newborns — displace bilirubin from albumin, immature glucuronidation — contraindicated in late pregnancy, nursing mothers and neonates; (5) hepatitis, nausea, vomiting, headache, depression; (6) drug interactions — displace warfarin, sulfonylureas (hypoglycaemia), methotrexate, phenytoin from protein binding and inhibit their metabolism; (7) cross-sensitivity with sulfonylureas, thiazides, furosemide, acetazolamide (sulfonamide moiety).",
[["Classification",[["Type","Drugs","Use"],["Short-acting","Sulfadiazine, sulfisoxazole","Toxoplasmosis"],["Intermediate","Sulfamethoxazole","With trimethoprim"],["Long-acting","Sulfadoxine","With pyrimethamine (malaria)"],["Topical","Silver sulfadiazine, mafenide, sulfacetamide","Burns, eye"],["Poorly absorbed","Sulfasalazine","UC, RA"]]],
["Adverse effects",["Crystalluria","Rash, SJS/TEN, fever","Haemolysis (G6PD deficiency)","Kernicterus (neonates)","Bone marrow suppression","Drug displacement interactions"]]],
{f:"PABA + pteridine → (dihydropteroate synthase ← sulfonamides) → dihydrofolate → (DHFR ← trimethoprim) → tetrahydrofolate → purines, DNA",c:"Sulfonamides inhibit bacterial folate synthesis; resistance and toxicity have limited their use, but they remain valuable in combinations (cotrimoxazole, SP) and topical formulations."});

Z("pharm10",2,"ph",["Cotrimoxazole is a fixed combination of trimethoprim and sulfamethoxazole (1:5) that blocks two consecutive steps of bacterial folate synthesis, producing a synergistic bactericidal effect; it is used for UTI, Pneumocystis pneumonia and other infections.","Two locks on the same door: sulfamethoxazole blocks one step, trimethoprim the next — together they kill rather than just slow bacteria.",["Ratio: 1:5 in formulation (80 mg TMP + 400 mg SMX; DS 160/800) → 1:20 in plasma (optimal synergy); similar t½ (~10 h).","MOA: sequential blockade — SMX inhibits dihydropteroate synthase, TMP inhibits bacterial DHFR (50,000× more than human).","Uses: UTI, Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (treatment + prophylaxis in HIV with CD4 < 200), toxoplasmosis prophylaxis, nocardiosis, shigellosis, typhoid (alternative), respiratory infections, Stenotrophomonas, MRSA skin infections, Whipple's, isosporiasis.","ADR: rash, SJS/TEN, nausea, megaloblastic anaemia (folate — give folinic acid), haemolysis (G6PD), bone marrow suppression, hyperkalaemia (TMP blocks ENaC), raised creatinine, kernicterus; more ADRs in AIDS patients."],"Cotrimoxazole: '1:5 tablet → 1:20 plasma'."],
"Cotrimoxazole is a fixed-dose combination of trimethoprim (TMP), a diaminopyrimidine, and sulfamethoxazole (SMX), an intermediate-acting sulfonamide, introduced in 1969. Rationale: SMX was chosen because its t½ (10 h) matches that of TMP (10 h). Optimal synergistic ratio against most organisms is 1:20 (TMP:SMX) in plasma; this is achieved by giving them in a 1:5 ratio (TMP 80 mg + SMX 400 mg; double strength 160 + 800 mg; paediatric 20 + 100 mg) because TMP has larger volume of distribution (enters tissues). Mechanism: sequential blockade of bacterial folate pathway — SMX inhibits dihydropteroate synthase (PABA → dihydrofolate), TMP inhibits dihydrofolate reductase (DHF → THF); TMP is about 50,000 times more potent against bacterial than human DHFR (selective toxicity); individually each is bacteriostatic, but the combination is bactericidal and synergistic, with reduced emergence of resistance. Spectrum: Gram-positive (Streptococcus pyogenes, S. pneumoniae, Staphylococcus aureus including many MRSA), Gram-negative (E. coli, Klebsiella, Proteus, Enterobacter, Salmonella, Shigella, H. influenzae, Moraxella, Neisseria, Stenotrophomonas maltophilia, Burkholderia cepacia), Nocardia, Listeria, Pneumocystis jirovecii, Toxoplasma, Isospora, Cyclospora; not Pseudomonas aeruginosa, anaerobes, Mycoplasma; resistance increasing in E. coli and Shigella. Pharmacokinetics: well absorbed orally, distributed widely (CSF, prostate — TMP concentrates in prostatic and vaginal fluid, sputum, bile), metabolised in liver (SMX acetylated), renal excretion; IV preparation available. Uses: (1) urinary tract infections — acute uncomplicated cystitis (3 days), recurrent UTI prophylaxis, chronic prostatitis (prostatic penetration); (2) Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia — drug of choice for treatment (high dose 15–20 mg/kg/day TMP IV/oral for 21 days with steroids in severe) and primary/secondary prophylaxis in HIV (CD4 < 200), transplant and chemotherapy patients (one DS daily); also prevents toxoplasmosis; in India, cotrimoxazole prophylaxis is part of NACO guidelines; (3) toxoplasmosis (treatment alternative, prophylaxis); (4) nocardiosis (drug of choice); (5) bacterial diarrhoeas — shigellosis (resistance), traveller's diarrhoea, cholera (alternative in children), isosporiasis and cyclosporiasis; (6) typhoid (when susceptible), (7) respiratory tract infections — acute exacerbations of chronic bronchitis, otitis media, sinusitis (limited by resistance); (8) skin and soft tissue infections including community MRSA; (9) Stenotrophomonas and Burkholderia infections, melioidosis maintenance; (10) chancroid, granuloma inguinale (donovanosis), Whipple disease (maintenance), brucellosis (with doxycycline in children), pertussis (macrolide allergy), listeriosis (penicillin allergy). Adverse effects: nausea, vomiting, glossitis, stomatitis; rashes, photosensitivity, Stevens–Johnson syndrome, TEN, exfoliative dermatitis (sulfonamide component; more frequent and severe in AIDS patients — up to 50%); megaloblastic anaemia, leucopenia, thrombocytopenia (folate deficiency — in folate-deficient, elderly, pregnant; prevented by folinic acid), haemolysis in G6PD deficiency; hyperkalaemia (TMP blocks ENaC like amiloride — especially with high doses, renal impairment, ACE inhibitors), raised serum creatinine (TMP inhibits tubular creatinine secretion), interstitial nephritis, crystalluria; hepatitis, aseptic meningitis, kernicterus in neonates; drug interactions — warfarin (raised INR), phenytoin, methotrexate (pancytopenia), sulfonylureas (hypoglycaemia), ACEI/spironolactone (hyperkalaemia), dofetilide. Contraindications: pregnancy (first trimester — antifolate; near term — kernicterus), neonates < 6 weeks, G6PD deficiency (caution), severe renal or hepatic disease, megaloblastic anaemia, sulfonamide allergy.",
[["Key features",[["Feature","Detail"],["Composition","TMP 80 mg + SMX 400 mg (1:5) → 1:20 in plasma"],["MOA","Sequential blockade of folate synthesis"],["Action","Bactericidal (synergy)"],["Not covered","Pseudomonas, anaerobes, Mycoplasma"],["Prostate penetration","Good (TMP)"]]],
["Uses",["UTI, prostatitis","Pneumocystis pneumonia (treatment + prophylaxis)","Toxoplasmosis prophylaxis","Nocardiosis (drug of choice)","Shigellosis, typhoid (if sensitive)","MRSA skin infections, Stenotrophomonas","Isospora, Cyclospora"]],
["Adverse effects",["Rash, SJS/TEN (esp. HIV)","Megaloblastic anaemia, leucopenia","Hyperkalaemia, ↑ creatinine","Haemolysis (G6PD)","Kernicterus","Drug interactions: warfarin, methotrexate, phenytoin"]]],
{c:"Cotrimoxazole's sequential blockade makes it bactericidal and broad spectrum; it remains the drug of choice for Pneumocystis pneumonia and nocardiosis, with hypersensitivity, haematological and hyperkalaemic toxicity requiring vigilance."});

Z("pharm10",3,"ph",["Fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin, moxifloxacin, ofloxacin) are synthetic bactericidal antibiotics that inhibit bacterial DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV; ciprofloxacin is especially active against Gram-negative bacteria including Pseudomonas.","DNA in bacteria is a tightly wound coil; gyrase unwinds it for copying — quinolones jam gyrase so DNA breaks and the bacterium dies.",["MOA: inhibit DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II — Gram-negative) and topoisomerase IV (Gram-positive) → DNA breaks → bactericidal; concentration-dependent killing, post-antibiotic effect.","Generations: 1st nalidixic acid (UTI only); 2nd ciprofloxacin, norfloxacin, ofloxacin (Gram −); 3rd levofloxacin (respiratory — pneumococcus); 4th moxifloxacin, gemifloxacin (anaerobes, respiratory, TB).","Ciprofloxacin uses: UTI, prostatitis, gonorrhoea (resistance), typhoid (resistance), bacterial diarrhoea, Pseudomonas infections, bone/joint, anthrax, plague, meningococcal prophylaxis; levo/moxi — CAP, MDR-TB.","ADR: GI upset, CNS (headache, dizziness, seizures, confusion), tendinitis/tendon rupture (elderly, steroids), QT prolongation, photosensitivity, dysglycaemia, aortic aneurysm, peripheral neuropathy, C. difficile; avoid in children/pregnancy (cartilage damage); interactions — antacids/iron/Ca (↓ absorption), theophylline (↑ levels — cipro inhibits CYP1A2), NSAIDs (seizures)."],"FQ ADR: 'tendons, QT, seizures, cartilage'."],
"Quinolones are synthetic antimicrobials; addition of fluorine at position 6 (fluoroquinolones) greatly increased potency and spectrum. Classification (generations): first — nalidixic acid, cinoxacin (narrow Gram-negative, urinary antiseptic); second — norfloxacin (urinary/GI only), ciprofloxacin, ofloxacin, pefloxacin, lomefloxacin (enhanced Gram-negative, Pseudomonas, atypicals); third — levofloxacin, sparfloxacin, gatifloxacin (improved Gram-positive especially Streptococcus pneumoniae — 'respiratory quinolones'); fourth — moxifloxacin, gemifloxacin, trovafloxacin, prulifloxacin, delafloxacin (added anaerobic activity, MRSA for delafloxacin). Mechanism: inhibit bacterial type II topoisomerases — DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II; introduces negative supercoils; main target in Gram-negative bacteria) by binding to the gyrase–DNA complex and stabilising DNA strand breaks; and topoisomerase IV (separates daughter chromosomes; main target in Gram-positive bacteria); result is fragmented DNA and rapid bactericidal effect; mammalian topoisomerase II is much less sensitive. Concentration-dependent killing (Cmax/MIC, AUC/MIC) and a significant post-antibiotic effect. Resistance: chromosomal mutations in gyrA/parC (target alteration), efflux pumps, reduced porins, plasmid-mediated qnr protection; widespread in Salmonella Typhi, gonococci, E. coli in India due to overuse. Ciprofloxacin: spectrum — highly active against Gram-negative bacilli (E. coli, Klebsiella, Enterobacter, Proteus, Salmonella including S. Typhi, Shigella, Campylobacter, Vibrio cholerae, Yersinia, H. influenzae, Moraxella, N. gonorrhoeae, N. meningitidis, Pseudomonas aeruginosa — most active quinolone), Brucella, Legionella, Mycoplasma, Chlamydia, Bacillus anthracis, M. tuberculosis (moderate); weak against Streptococcus pneumoniae and anaerobes. Pharmacokinetics: oral bioavailability 70–80% (chelated by divalent/trivalent cations — antacids, iron, calcium, zinc, sucralfate, dairy — separate by 2–6 h), wide tissue penetration (prostate, bone, lung, kidney, bile, macrophages; CSF moderate), t½ 3–5 h, excreted mainly by kidneys (dose adjustment), partly hepatic; IV preparation. Uses: (1) urinary tract infections — complicated UTI, pyelonephritis, prostatitis (excellent penetration); avoid for uncomplicated cystitis (stewardship); (2) gonorrhoea (no longer first-line due to resistance; ceftriaxone preferred), chancroid; (3) bacterial gastroenteritis and diarrhoea — shigellosis, Campylobacter, traveller's diarrhoea, cholera (single dose in adults); (4) typhoid fever — drug of choice historically; now high resistance in South Asia (nalidixic-acid-resistant strains) — azithromycin, ceftriaxone preferred; (5) bone and joint infections (Gram-negative osteomyelitis), skin and soft tissue infections, diabetic foot (with anti-Gram-positive agent); (6) Pseudomonas infections — with β-lactam; cystic fibrosis exacerbation; malignant otitis externa; (7) respiratory infections — Gram-negative pneumonia, exacerbations of COPD; levofloxacin and moxifloxacin preferred for community-acquired pneumonia (pneumococcus, atypicals); (8) tuberculosis — levofloxacin, moxifloxacin in MDR-TB regimens; (9) prophylaxis — meningococcal contacts (single dose 500 mg), neutropenic patients, inhalational anthrax post-exposure, plague; (10) intra-abdominal infections (with metronidazole), febrile neutropenia; (11) topical — conjunctivitis, keratitis, otitis (ciprofloxacin, ofloxacin, moxifloxacin drops). Adverse effects: GI — nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, abdominal pain, C. difficile colitis; CNS — headache, dizziness, insomnia, restlessness, anxiety, confusion, hallucinations, seizures (especially with NSAIDs or theophylline), peripheral neuropathy (may be permanent), myasthenia gravis exacerbation; musculoskeletal — tendinitis and Achilles tendon rupture (risk with age > 60, corticosteroids, renal or transplant patients), arthralgia, arthropathy and cartilage damage in growing animals (avoid in children and adolescents except specific indications such as cystic fibrosis, anthrax, complicated UTI); cardiovascular — QT prolongation and torsades (moxifloxacin, gatifloxacin, sparfloxacin > levofloxacin > ciprofloxacin), aortic aneurysm/dissection; dysglycaemia — hypo- and hyperglycaemia (especially gatifloxacin — withdrawn; elderly diabetics); skin — rashes, photosensitivity (sparfloxacin, lomefloxacin); hepatotoxicity (trovafloxacin withdrawn); crystalluria (alkaline urine); hypersensitivity. FDA boxed warnings restrict use in uncomplicated infections when alternatives exist. Drug interactions: ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP1A2 — raises theophylline (seizures, arrhythmia), caffeine, warfarin, tizanidine, clozapine levels; NSAIDs increase CNS stimulation and seizures; antacids, iron, calcium, zinc, sucralfate, didanosine reduce absorption; QT-prolonging drugs additive. Contraindications: pregnancy, lactation, children (relative), history of tendon disorders with FQ, epilepsy (caution), myasthenia gravis, QT prolongation.",
[["Generations",[["Generation","Drugs","Key spectrum"],["1st","Nalidixic acid","Gram-negative (UTI)"],["2nd","Ciprofloxacin, norfloxacin, ofloxacin","Gram-negative incl. Pseudomonas"],["3rd","Levofloxacin","+ Pneumococcus, atypicals"],["4th","Moxifloxacin, gemifloxacin","+ Anaerobes, respiratory, TB"]]],
["Uses of ciprofloxacin",["Complicated UTI, prostatitis","Bacterial diarrhoea, cholera","Typhoid (if sensitive)","Pseudomonas infections","Bone, joint, soft tissue infections","Meningococcal, anthrax, plague prophylaxis","Eye and ear drops"]],
["Adverse effects",["GI upset, C. difficile","CNS: dizziness, seizures, confusion","Tendinitis, tendon rupture","QT prolongation","Cartilage damage (children)","Photosensitivity, dysglycaemia","Peripheral neuropathy, aortic aneurysm"]],
["Interactions",[["Interacting drug","Effect"],["Antacids, iron, calcium, zinc","↓ Absorption"],["Theophylline, caffeine","↑ Levels (CYP1A2 inhibition)"],["NSAIDs","↑ Seizure risk"],["Warfarin","↑ INR"],["QT drugs","Torsades"]]]],
{i:"Fluoroquinolones are potent oral bactericidal drugs but overuse has driven resistance and serious adverse effects; they are best reserved for specific indications.",f:"Ciprofloxacin → enters bacterium (porins)\n → binds DNA gyrase (Gram −) / topoisomerase IV (Gram +)\n → stabilises cleaved DNA complex → double-strand breaks → cell death\n Resistance: gyrA/parC mutations, efflux, qnr",c:"Ciprofloxacin is a broad Gram-negative bactericidal fluoroquinolone with excellent tissue penetration; resistance, tendinopathy, CNS, QT and dysglycaemic risks demand judicious, stewardship-based use."});

Z("pharm10",4,"ph",["Typhoid (enteric) fever, caused by Salmonella Typhi and Paratyphi, is treated with antibiotics chosen according to local resistance: ceftriaxone or azithromycin are first-line in India because of widespread fluoroquinolone resistance.","Old warhorses (chloramphenicol, ampicillin, cotrimoxazole) and then ciprofloxacin lost to resistance; today ceftriaxone (IV) and azithromycin (oral) lead, with meropenem for XDR strains.",["Uncomplicated: azithromycin 20 mg/kg/day (500 mg–1 g) × 7 days; or cefixime 20 mg/kg/day × 14 days; or (if sensitive) chloramphenicol, amoxicillin, cotrimoxazole; ofloxacin/ciprofloxacin only if susceptible.","Severe/complicated (inpatient): ceftriaxone 2 g/day (75 mg/kg children) IV × 10–14 days (± azithromycin).","XDR typhoid (Pakistan strain — resistant to ceftriaxone): meropenem ± azithromycin.","Supportive: fluids, antipyretics (paracetamol), nutrition; dexamethasone for severe typhoid with delirium/shock; chronic carrier — ciprofloxacin 4 weeks or cholecystectomy; prevention — Vi-TCV vaccine."],"Typhoid now: 'Ceftriaxone IV or Azithromycin oral'."],
"Enteric fever is a systemic infection caused by Salmonella enterica serovar Typhi (and Paratyphi A, B, C), endemic in South Asia including India. Antimicrobial history and resistance: chloramphenicol (1948) was the mainstay until plasmid-mediated resistance; multidrug-resistant (MDR) strains (resistant to chloramphenicol, ampicillin and cotrimoxazole) emerged in the 1980s–90s; fluoroquinolones then became drugs of choice, but decreased ciprofloxacin susceptibility (nalidixic acid–resistant strains) and full resistance are now widespread in India; extensively drug-resistant (XDR) strains (MDR + fluoroquinolone + third-generation cephalosporin resistance; ESBL) emerged in Pakistan (2016); azithromycin resistance reported sporadically; interestingly, susceptibility to chloramphenicol, ampicillin and cotrimoxazole has partially returned in some areas. Treatment recommendations (Indian guidelines/ICMR, WHO): (1) uncomplicated enteric fever (outpatient) — azithromycin 10–20 mg/kg/day (adults 500 mg–1 g) for 7 days (drug of choice orally; good intracellular penetration; low relapse), or cefixime 15–20 mg/kg/day in two doses for 10–14 days (higher relapse rates); alternatives when susceptible — chloramphenicol 50–75 mg/kg/day × 14 days, amoxicillin 75–100 mg/kg/day × 14 days, cotrimoxazole × 14 days; ciprofloxacin or ofloxacin only if culture shows full susceptibility (MIC ≤ 0.06); (2) severe or complicated enteric fever (inability to take orally, persistent vomiting, toxaemia, GI bleeding, perforation, encephalopathy, shock) — ceftriaxone 2–4 g/day IV (children 75–100 mg/kg/day) for 10–14 days or cefotaxime; combination of ceftriaxone + azithromycin in severe disease or slow response; (3) XDR typhoid — meropenem (1 g 8-hourly) or other carbapenems, with azithromycin for uncomplicated XDR cases; tigecycline in some; (4) relapse — treat as primary episode based on sensitivity; (5) chronic carriers (> 1 year excretion) — ciprofloxacin 750 mg BD for 4 weeks (if susceptible) or amoxicillin/cotrimoxazole for 6 weeks; cholecystectomy if gallstones; (6) adjuncts — high-dose dexamethasone (3 mg/kg then 1 mg/kg 6-hourly × 48 h) for severe typhoid with delirium, obtundation, coma or shock (reduces mortality); antipyretics (paracetamol; avoid NSAIDs — bleeding, perforation), fluids, nutrition, nursing; surgery for perforation (with metronidazole and broader cover). Fever defervescence is slow (4–7 days) even with effective therapy — not a reason to change drug before 5 days unless deteriorating. Prevention: safe water, sanitation, food hygiene, handwashing; vaccines — typhoid conjugate vaccine (Vi-TT; single dose from 6 months; included in some state programmes), Vi polysaccharide (> 2 years; booster every 3 years), oral live Ty21a.",
[["Treatment regimens",[["Situation","First choice","Alternatives"],["Uncomplicated (oral)","Azithromycin × 7 days","Cefixime × 14 days; chloramphenicol/amoxicillin/cotrimoxazole if sensitive"],["Severe/complicated","Ceftriaxone IV × 10–14 days","Cefotaxime; + azithromycin"],["XDR typhoid","Meropenem ± azithromycin","Tigecycline"],["Chronic carrier","Ciprofloxacin 4 weeks (if sensitive)","Amoxicillin/cotrimoxazole 6 weeks; cholecystectomy"]]],
["Adjuncts",["Dexamethasone for severe typhoid with delirium/shock","Paracetamol (avoid NSAIDs)","Fluids, nutrition","Surgery + metronidazole for perforation"]]],
{c:"Typhoid treatment has evolved with resistance: azithromycin orally and ceftriaxone parenterally are now standard in India, with carbapenems for XDR strains and vaccination for prevention."});

Z("pharm10",5,"ph",["Penicillins are bactericidal β-lactam antibiotics that inhibit bacterial cell wall synthesis; natural penicillin G is narrow spectrum, while semisynthetic penicillins add acid stability (penicillin V), β-lactamase resistance (cloxacillin), broader spectrum (ampicillin, amoxicillin) or anti-Pseudomonas activity (piperacillin).","Penicillin stops bacteria building their wall bricks into a mesh (cross-linking); growing bacteria burst like over-inflated balloons.",["MOA: β-lactam binds penicillin-binding proteins (transpeptidases) → inhibits peptidoglycan cross-linking → autolysins → lysis; bactericidal, time-dependent.","Penicillin G uses: streptococcal pharyngitis, rheumatic fever prophylaxis (benzathine penicillin), syphilis (drug of choice), pneumococcal, meningococcal infections, diphtheria, tetanus, gas gangrene, anthrax, leptospirosis, actinomycosis.","Semisynthetic: acid-resistant (penicillin V); penicillinase-resistant (methicillin, cloxacillin, dicloxacillin, nafcillin, oxacillin); extended (ampicillin, amoxicillin); antipseudomonal (carbenicillin, ticarcillin, piperacillin); with β-lactamase inhibitors.","ADR: hypersensitivity (rash to anaphylaxis — skin test; adrenaline), Jarisch–Herxheimer reaction (syphilis), seizures (high dose/intrathecal), hyperkalaemia (K⁺ salt), ampicillin rash (EBV), diarrhoea; ampicillin uses — UTI, respiratory, typhoid, meningitis (Listeria), enterococcal infections, H. influenzae."],"Penicillin G = syphilis drug of choice; ampicillin rash in infectious mononucleosis."],
"Penicillins are β-lactam antibiotics derived from 6-aminopenicillanic acid (thiazolidine ring fused to β-lactam ring); penicillin G was discovered by Fleming (1928) from Penicillium notatum. Mechanism: bacterial cell wall contains peptidoglycan — glycan chains (NAM–NAG) cross-linked by peptide bridges; final cross-linking (transpeptidation) is catalysed by penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs — transpeptidases, carboxypeptidases); β-lactams structurally resemble D-alanyl-D-alanine and acylate PBPs, inhibiting transpeptidation; the defective wall cannot resist osmotic pressure, and activation of autolysins causes lysis — bactericidal against actively multiplying bacteria (time-dependent killing; T > MIC). Selective toxicity — mammalian cells lack cell walls. Resistance: β-lactamase (penicillinase) production (staphylococci, gonococci, H. influenzae, Gram-negative bacilli; ESBLs, carbapenemases), altered PBPs with low affinity (MRSA — PBP2a encoded by mecA; penicillin-resistant pneumococci), reduced permeability (porin loss), efflux pumps. Classification: (1) natural penicillins — benzylpenicillin (penicillin G; sodium/potassium salts IV/IM), long-acting repository forms — procaine penicillin, benzathine penicillin (2.4 MU IM gives low levels for 3–4 weeks); (2) semisynthetic — (a) acid-resistant: phenoxymethylpenicillin (penicillin V); (b) penicillinase-resistant (antistaphylococcal): methicillin, cloxacillin, dicloxacillin, flucloxacillin, oxacillin, nafcillin; (c) extended spectrum aminopenicillins: ampicillin, bacampicillin, amoxicillin; (d) antipseudomonal: carboxypenicillins — carbenicillin, ticarcillin; ureidopenicillins — piperacillin, mezlocillin, azlocillin; (e) β-lactamase inhibitor combinations — amoxicillin–clavulanate, ampicillin–sulbactam, piperacillin–tazobactam, ticarcillin–clavulanate. Penicillin G: spectrum — narrow; Gram-positive cocci (Streptococcus pyogenes, viridans streptococci, S. pneumoniae — resistance emerging, non-penicillinase-producing staphylococci), Gram-negative cocci (Neisseria meningitidis, N. gonorrhoeae — mostly resistant now), Gram-positive bacilli (Corynebacterium diphtheriae, Bacillus anthracis, Clostridium tetani, C. perfringens, Listeria), spirochaetes (Treponema pallidum, Leptospira, Borrelia), Actinomyces, Pasteurella; most anaerobes except Bacteroides fragilis. Pharmacokinetics: acid-labile (destroyed in stomach — given parenterally), short t½ (30 min), rapid renal excretion by tubular secretion (90%) — prolonged by probenecid; poor CSF penetration unless meninges inflamed; distributed in extracellular fluid. Uses: streptococcal infections — pharyngitis, tonsillitis, scarlet fever, erysipelas, cellulitis, puerperal sepsis; prophylaxis of rheumatic fever (benzathine penicillin 1.2 MU IM every 3–4 weeks — secondary prophylaxis); infective endocarditis due to viridans streptococci (with gentamicin); pneumococcal pneumonia and meningitis (if sensitive); meningococcal meningitis; syphilis — drug of choice (benzathine penicillin 2.4 MU IM single dose for early syphilis; weekly × 3 for late; aqueous penicillin G for neurosyphilis and congenital syphilis); yaws; diphtheria (with antitoxin), tetanus (with antitoxin), gas gangrene (high dose with surgery), anthrax, actinomycosis, leptospirosis, Lyme disease, rat bite fever, Listeria (with gentamicin); prophylaxis — rheumatic fever, splenectomised patients, before dental procedures (amoxicillin). Adverse effects: (1) hypersensitivity (most important; 1–10%) — urticaria, rash, fever, angioedema, bronchospasm, serum sickness, exfoliative dermatitis, Stevens–Johnson syndrome, interstitial nephritis, haemolytic anaemia, anaphylactic shock (0.002–0.05%; fatal); cross-reactivity with cephalosporins (~1–2% for later generations) and carbapenems (< 1%); management — history, skin testing (scratch/intradermal with benzylpenicilloyl polylysine and minor determinants), keep adrenaline ready, desensitisation when essential (syphilis in pregnancy); treatment of anaphylaxis — adrenaline 0.5 mg IM (1:1000), antihistamine, hydrocortisone, fluids, oxygen; (2) local — pain at IM site, thrombophlebitis, sterile abscess; nerve injury; (3) neurotoxicity — seizures with very high doses, intrathecal injection or renal failure; (4) Jarisch–Herxheimer reaction in syphilis (fever, chills, myalgia, hypotension within hours due to endotoxin release from killed spirochaetes); (5) electrolyte — hyperkalaemia with large doses of potassium penicillin G in renal failure, sodium overload (carbenicillin); (6) Hoigné syndrome (procaine reaction — psychotic episode), accidental IV injection of benzathine/procaine (embolic phenomena); (7) superinfection, diarrhoea. Ampicillin: aminopenicillin; acid-stable; spectrum of penicillin G plus Gram-negative bacilli — H. influenzae, E. coli, Proteus mirabilis, Salmonella, Shigella, also Enterococcus faecalis and Listeria; destroyed by penicillinase (inactive against β-lactamase producers — ~50% E. coli, most Klebsiella, staphylococci); oral bioavailability 40% (reduced by food — amoxicillin better absorbed 90%, less diarrhoea); t½ 1 h; renal and biliary excretion. Uses: respiratory infections — sinusitis, otitis media, bronchitis, community-acquired pneumonia (amoxicillin first-line in children and adults); urinary tract infections (resistance common); meningitis — neonatal and Listeria meningitis (ampicillin + gentamicin/cefotaxime), H. influenzae (if sensitive); typhoid fever (if sensitive; carriers); bacillary dysentery (resistance); enterococcal infections and endocarditis (with gentamicin); gonorrhoea (non-penicillinase strains); H. pylori eradication (amoxicillin); prophylaxis of infective endocarditis before dental procedures (amoxicillin 2 g single dose); Lyme disease, anthrax, leptospirosis. Adverse effects: diarrhoea (frequent with ampicillin; C. difficile), non-allergic maculopapular rash — especially in infectious mononucleosis (EBV; 80–100%), CMV infection, chronic lymphocytic leukaemia, or with allopurinol (not a true allergy); hypersensitivity reactions as for other penicillins.",
[["Classification",[["Group","Drugs","Key feature"],["Natural","Penicillin G, procaine, benzathine penicillin","Narrow spectrum; acid-labile"],["Acid-resistant","Penicillin V","Oral"],["Penicillinase-resistant","Cloxacillin, dicloxacillin, flucloxacillin, methicillin, nafcillin","Anti-staphylococcal"],["Extended spectrum","Ampicillin, amoxicillin","+ Gram-negative (H. influenzae, E. coli)"],["Antipseudomonal","Carbenicillin, ticarcillin, piperacillin","Pseudomonas"],["With β-lactamase inhibitor","Amoxicillin–clavulanate, piperacillin–tazobactam","Overcome β-lactamases"]]],
["Uses of penicillin G",["Streptococcal infections; rheumatic fever prophylaxis","Syphilis (drug of choice), yaws","Pneumococcal, meningococcal infections","Diphtheria, tetanus, gas gangrene","Anthrax, actinomycosis, leptospirosis","Viridans/enterococcal endocarditis (with gentamicin)"]],
["Adverse effects",[["ADR","Note"],["Hypersensitivity","Rash to anaphylaxis; skin test; adrenaline"],["Jarisch–Herxheimer","Syphilis treatment"],["Seizures","High dose, renal failure, intrathecal"],["Hyperkalaemia","K⁺ penicillin in renal failure"],["Ampicillin rash","Infectious mononucleosis, allopurinol"],["Diarrhoea, superinfection","Ampicillin > amoxicillin"]]],
["Penicillin G vs ampicillin",[["Feature","Penicillin G","Ampicillin"],["Acid stability","No","Yes"],["Spectrum","Narrow (Gram +, spirochaetes)","Extended (+ H. influenzae, E. coli, Salmonella, Enterococcus)"],["Route","IV/IM","Oral, IV"],["Penicillinase","Destroyed","Destroyed"],["Special ADR","Jarisch–Herxheimer","Rash in EBV, diarrhoea"]]]],
{i:"Penicillins remain among the safest and most effective antibiotics; semisynthetic modifications overcame acid lability, narrow spectrum and β-lactamase susceptibility.",f:"Penicillin → binds PBPs (transpeptidase)\n → blocks peptidoglycan cross-linking → weak wall\n → autolysin activation → lysis (bactericidal)\n Resistance: β-lactamase, altered PBP (MRSA), ↓ permeability",c:"Penicillin G remains the drug of choice for syphilis and streptococcal infections, while semisynthetic penicillins such as ampicillin/amoxicillin extend cover to Gram-negative organisms; hypersensitivity is the most important adverse effect."});

Z("pharm10",6,"ph",["β-lactamase inhibitors (clavulanic acid, sulbactam, tazobactam, avibactam) bind and inactivate bacterial β-lactamase enzymes, protecting partner β-lactam antibiotics and restoring their activity against resistant bacteria.","β-lactamase is the bacteria's bodyguard that destroys penicillin; clavulanic acid is a decoy that sacrifices itself and disables the bodyguard.",["Clavulanic acid: from Streptomyces clavuligerus; 'suicide' (irreversible) inhibitor of class A β-lactamases (penicillinases, many ESBLs); weak antibacterial itself.","Combinations: amoxicillin + clavulanate (co-amoxiclav), ticarcillin + clavulanate; ampicillin + sulbactam; piperacillin + tazobactam; cefoperazone + sulbactam; ceftazidime + avibactam, meropenem + vaborbactam, imipenem + relebactam (newer, KPC carbapenemases).","Co-amoxiclav uses: β-lactamase-producing H. influenzae, Moraxella, S. aureus (MSSA), E. coli, Klebsiella, anaerobes (B. fragilis) — sinusitis, otitis, LRTI, UTI, skin/soft tissue, animal/human bites, dental and intra-abdominal infections.","ADR: diarrhoea, nausea, cholestatic hepatitis (clavulanate — elderly, men, prolonged), rash, candidiasis; not effective against MRSA, AmpC, metallo-β-lactamases (except newer agents partly)."],"Clavulanate = 'suicide inhibitor'."],
"β-lactamases are bacterial enzymes that hydrolyse the β-lactam ring, inactivating penicillins and cephalosporins — the most important mechanism of β-lactam resistance (staphylococcal penicillinase, TEM/SHV enzymes of Gram-negative bacilli, extended-spectrum β-lactamases, AmpC, carbapenemases — KPC, OXA-48, NDM metallo-β-lactamases). β-lactamase inhibitors are β-lactam-like molecules (or non-β-lactams like avibactam) with little intrinsic antibacterial activity that bind β-lactamases and inactivate them, so the partner antibiotic is protected and reaches its PBP targets. Clavulanic acid: obtained from Streptomyces clavuligerus; a 'progressive' or 'suicide' inhibitor — it binds the active site serine of the enzyme, is cleaved, and forms a stable acyl-enzyme complex that irreversibly inactivates it; active against class A β-lactamases (staphylococcal penicillinase, plasmid-mediated TEM-1, SHV, many ESBLs) and those of H. influenzae, Moraxella, N. gonorrhoeae, Bacteroides; poorly active against chromosomal AmpC (class C, Enterobacter, Citrobacter, Pseudomonas) and metallo-β-lactamases (class B); pharmacokinetics similar to amoxicillin (well absorbed orally, t½ 1 h) — allowing combination. Sulbactam (penicillanic acid sulfone; also active against Acinetobacter baumannii intrinsically) and tazobactam (similar, broader) are other β-lactam-based inhibitors. Newer non-β-lactam inhibitors: avibactam (diazabicyclooctane; inhibits class A including KPC, class C AmpC and some class D OXA-48), relebactam, vaborbactam (boronic acid; KPC), durlobactam; none inhibit metallo-β-lactamases (NDM) — aztreonam–avibactam combination used. Combinations: amoxicillin–clavulanic acid (co-amoxiclav; 4:1 to 14:1 oral, 5:1 IV), ticarcillin–clavulanic acid, ampicillin–sulbactam, cefoperazone–sulbactam, piperacillin–tazobactam (8:1; broad spectrum including Pseudomonas and anaerobes — empirical therapy for nosocomial infections, febrile neutropenia, intra-abdominal sepsis), ceftolozane–tazobactam (MDR Pseudomonas), ceftazidime–avibactam (KPC/OXA-48 CRE), meropenem–vaborbactam, imipenem–relebactam, sulbactam–durlobactam (Acinetobacter). Amoxicillin–clavulanate uses: infections due to β-lactamase-producing organisms — acute otitis media, sinusitis, tonsillitis (recurrent), lower respiratory tract infections (exacerbation of COPD, community-acquired pneumonia), urinary tract infections (including in pregnancy), skin and soft tissue infections, animal and human bite wounds (Pasteurella, anaerobes, staphylococci), dental abscesses, diabetic foot (mild), intra-abdominal and gynaecological infections (with anaerobes), gonorrhoea (β-lactamase strains), chancroid, H. pylori (amoxicillin component), surgical prophylaxis. Adverse effects: diarrhoea (more than amoxicillin alone), nausea, vomiting, abdominal discomfort (take with food), rashes and hypersensitivity (penicillin allergy), vaginal candidiasis, cholestatic hepatitis and jaundice (attributed to clavulanate; more common in elderly men and after prolonged courses; may appear after stopping), C. difficile colitis, rarely Stevens–Johnson syndrome, interstitial nephritis. Contraindications: penicillin allergy, previous co-amoxiclav–associated jaundice; dose adjustment in renal impairment.",
[["Combinations",[["Combination","Main use"],["Amoxicillin + clavulanate","Respiratory, UTI, bites, dental, skin"],["Ampicillin + sulbactam","Mixed infections, Acinetobacter"],["Piperacillin + tazobactam","Nosocomial sepsis, febrile neutropenia, Pseudomonas"],["Cefoperazone + sulbactam","Hospital infections"],["Ceftazidime + avibactam","Carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales (KPC, OXA-48)"],["Meropenem + vaborbactam","KPC producers"]]],
["Spectrum of inhibition",[["β-lactamase class","Clavulanate/sulbactam/tazobactam","Avibactam"],["A (penicillinases, ESBL)","Yes","Yes"],["A (KPC carbapenemase)","No","Yes"],["C (AmpC)","Poor","Yes"],["D (OXA-48)","Variable","Yes"],["B (metallo, NDM)","No","No"]]]],
{c:"β-lactamase inhibitors restore the activity of β-lactams against enzyme-producing bacteria; co-amoxiclav is widely used for community infections and newer inhibitors combat carbapenem-resistant organisms."});

Z("pharm10",7,"ph",["Cephalosporins are β-lactam antibiotics related to penicillins, grouped into five generations with increasing Gram-negative cover; ceftriaxone, a third-generation drug, is widely used for serious infections such as meningitis, typhoid, gonorrhoea and sepsis.","Each new generation of cephalosporin learnt to fight more Gram-negative bacteria, often trading some Gram-positive power; the 5th generation even beats MRSA.",["1st (cefazolin, cephalexin, cefadroxil): Gram + (staph, strep); surgical prophylaxis, skin infections.","2nd (cefuroxime, cefaclor, cefoxitin, cefotetan): + H. influenzae, Moraxella, anaerobes (cephamycins); respiratory infections.","3rd (ceftriaxone, cefotaxime, ceftazidime — Pseudomonas, cefoperazone, cefixime, cefpodoxime oral): strong Gram −, CNS penetration; 4th (cefepime, cefpirome): Gram + and Gram − including Pseudomonas, AmpC stable; 5th (ceftaroline, ceftobiprole): MRSA; siderophore cefiderocol (carbapenem-resistant).","Ceftriaxone: long t½ (8 h — once daily), biliary + renal excretion (no renal dose adjustment), crosses BBB; uses — meningitis, typhoid, gonorrhoea (500 mg–1 g IM single), CAP, sepsis, Lyme, chancroid, surgical prophylaxis; ADR — hypersensitivity, biliary sludge/pseudolithiasis, neonatal kernicterus (bilirubin displacement), Ca²⁺ precipitation in neonates (don't mix with calcium IV), C. difficile, disulfiram-like (cefoperazone), bleeding (MTT side chain)."],"Ceftriaxone 'once daily, biliary excretion, gonorrhoea + meningitis + typhoid'."],
"Cephalosporins are β-lactam antibiotics derived from cephalosporin C (Cephalosporium acremonium); nucleus 7-aminocephalosporanic acid (β-lactam ring fused with dihydrothiazine ring) — modifications at position 7 alter antibacterial activity and at position 3 alter pharmacokinetics. Mechanism: like penicillins — bind PBPs, inhibit transpeptidation of peptidoglycan, bactericidal; generally more resistant to staphylococcal penicillinase; enterococci, Listeria, MRSA (except 5th generation), atypicals are intrinsically resistant. Resistance: β-lactamases (cephalosporinases, ESBLs, AmpC, carbapenemases), altered PBPs, reduced permeability. Classification (generations): first generation — parenteral: cefazolin, cephalothin; oral: cephalexin, cefadroxil, cephradine; highly active against Gram-positive cocci (MSSA, streptococci) and some Gram-negatives (E. coli, Klebsiella, Proteus mirabilis); uses — surgical prophylaxis (cefazolin), skin and soft tissue infections, UTI, streptococcal pharyngitis (penicillin allergy without anaphylaxis); second generation — parenteral: cefuroxime, cefoxitin, cefotetan, cefamandole; oral: cefaclor, cefuroxime axetil, cefprozil; increased activity against H. influenzae, Moraxella, Neisseria, Enterobacteriaceae; cephamycins (cefoxitin, cefotetan) active against anaerobes (B. fragilis); uses — respiratory infections (sinusitis, otitis, bronchitis, CAP), UTI, gonorrhoea, mixed aerobic–anaerobic infections (cefoxitin — pelvic, intra-abdominal), surgical prophylaxis; third generation — parenteral: cefotaxime, ceftriaxone, ceftazidime, cefoperazone, ceftizoxime; oral: cefixime, cefpodoxime proxetil, cefdinir, ceftibuten; markedly increased activity against Gram-negative bacilli (Enterobacteriaceae, H. influenzae, Neisseria, Salmonella), reduced activity against Gram-positives (except ceftriaxone/cefotaxime against pneumococci), good CNS penetration; ceftazidime and cefoperazone active against Pseudomonas aeruginosa; uses — meningitis, typhoid, gonorrhoea, nosocomial and serious Gram-negative infections, septicaemia, CAP (hospitalised), complicated UTI, febrile neutropenia (ceftazidime), peritonitis, Lyme disease; fourth generation — cefepime, cefpirome: broad spectrum against Gram-positive and Gram-negative including Pseudomonas and many Enterobacter; more resistant to AmpC β-lactamases, better penetration through Gram-negative outer membrane (zwitterion); uses — serious hospital infections, febrile neutropenia; neurotoxicity (encephalopathy, non-convulsive status) in renal failure; fifth generation — ceftaroline fosamil, ceftobiprole: active against MRSA (binds PBP2a) and penicillin-resistant pneumococci plus Gram-negatives (not Pseudomonas for ceftaroline); uses — complicated skin infections, CAP; newer — ceftolozane–tazobactam, ceftazidime–avibactam, cefiderocol (siderophore cephalosporin using bacterial iron transport — carbapenem-resistant Gram-negatives). Ceftriaxone: third-generation parenteral cephalosporin. Spectrum — Gram-negative: E. coli, Klebsiella, Proteus, Salmonella Typhi, Shigella, H. influenzae (including β-lactamase producers), N. gonorrhoeae (including penicillinase producers), N. meningitidis, Moraxella; Gram-positive: S. pneumoniae (including many penicillin-intermediate), S. pyogenes, MSSA (moderate); spirochaetes (Borrelia, Treponema); not Pseudomonas, Enterococcus, Listeria, MRSA, anaerobes (B. fragilis), atypicals. Pharmacokinetics — IV or IM; highly protein bound (85–95%); longest t½ among cephalosporins (6–9 h) allowing once-daily dosing; excellent penetration into tissues including CSF with inflamed meninges; dual elimination — 40% biliary (high biliary concentration) and 60% renal — no dose adjustment in renal failure alone. Dose: 1–2 g once daily (up to 2 g 12-hourly in meningitis); children 50–100 mg/kg/day. Uses: (1) bacterial meningitis — empirical therapy (with vancomycin ± ampicillin) for pneumococcal, meningococcal and H. influenzae; chemoprophylaxis of meningococcal contacts (250 mg IM single dose); (2) typhoid fever — severe/complicated (drug of choice); (3) gonorrhoea — single 500 mg–1 g IM dose (drug of choice; with azithromycin/doxycycline for chlamydia), disseminated gonococcal infection, gonococcal ophthalmia neonatorum; chancroid (single 250 mg IM); (4) community-acquired pneumonia (hospitalised; with macrolide), lower respiratory infections; (5) septicaemia, complicated UTI, pyelonephritis, intra-abdominal infections (with metronidazole), bone and joint infections, skin and soft tissue infections; (6) Lyme disease (neuroborreliosis, carditis), neurosyphilis (alternative); (7) surgical prophylaxis; (8) spontaneous bacterial peritonitis prophylaxis in variceal bleeding; (9) acute otitis media (single/3-day IM in children); (10) outpatient parenteral antibiotic therapy (once daily). Adverse effects: hypersensitivity — rash, fever, eosinophilia, urticaria, anaphylaxis (cross-reactivity with penicillin ~1–2%; avoid in history of penicillin anaphylaxis); pain at IM site (reconstitute with lignocaine), thrombophlebitis; GI — diarrhoea, C. difficile colitis; biliary sludge and pseudolithiasis (calcium–ceftriaxone precipitate in gallbladder; reversible), renal stones; neonatal hyperbilirubinaemia and kernicterus (displaces bilirubin — avoid in hyperbilirubinaemic and premature neonates); fatal precipitation with calcium-containing IV solutions in neonates (do not co-administer with calcium in neonates; flush lines in older patients); haemolytic anaemia (immune), neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, raised liver enzymes; superinfection (Candida). Other cephalosporin-specific: cefoperazone, cefotetan, cefamandole contain N-methylthiotetrazole (MTT) side chain — hypoprothrombinaemia (bleeding) and disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol; cefepime neurotoxicity; nephrotoxicity of cephaloridine (obsolete) increased with aminoglycosides and loop diuretics.",
[["Generations",[["Generation","Drugs","Spectrum / use"],["1st","Cefazolin, cephalexin, cefadroxil","Gram + (staph, strep); surgical prophylaxis"],["2nd","Cefuroxime, cefaclor, cefoxitin, cefotetan","+ H. influenzae, Moraxella; anaerobes (cephamycins)"],["3rd","Ceftriaxone, cefotaxime, ceftazidime, cefoperazone, cefixime, cefpodoxime","Strong Gram −; CNS; ceftazidime → Pseudomonas"],["4th","Cefepime, cefpirome","Gram + and Gram − incl. Pseudomonas; AmpC stable"],["5th","Ceftaroline, ceftobiprole","+ MRSA"],["Newer","Cefiderocol, ceftolozane–tazobactam, ceftazidime–avibactam","MDR Gram-negatives"]]],
["Ceftriaxone profile",[["Aspect","Detail"],["t½","6–9 h (once daily)"],["Excretion","Biliary + renal (no renal adjustment)"],["CNS","Good penetration (meningitis)"],["Gaps","Pseudomonas, Enterococcus, Listeria, MRSA, anaerobes"],["Key ADR","Hypersensitivity, biliary sludge, neonatal kernicterus, Ca²⁺ precipitation, C. difficile"]]],
["Uses of ceftriaxone",["Bacterial meningitis","Severe typhoid","Gonorrhoea, chancroid","CAP, sepsis, complicated UTI","Lyme disease, neurosyphilis","Surgical prophylaxis, SBP prophylaxis"]]],
{i:"Cephalosporins are the most widely used class of antibiotics in hospitals; generations reflect evolving spectra.",f:"Generation ↑ → Gram-negative activity ↑ (1st → 4th), Gram-positive ↓ (except 4th, 5th)\n 3rd: ceftriaxone (meningitis, typhoid, gonorrhoea), ceftazidime (Pseudomonas)\n 4th: cefepime (broad, AmpC stable)\n 5th: ceftaroline (MRSA)",c:"Cephalosporins provide progressively broader Gram-negative cover across generations; ceftriaxone, with once-daily dosing, CNS penetration and dual excretion, is a workhorse for meningitis, typhoid, gonorrhoea and sepsis."});

Z("pharm10",8,"ph",["Tetracyclines (doxycycline, minocycline, tetracycline) are broad-spectrum bacteriostatic antibiotics that block bacterial protein synthesis at the 30S ribosome; doxycycline is used for rickettsial infections, cholera, chlamydia, acne, malaria prophylaxis and more.","They stop the tRNA delivery truck docking at the ribosome — no amino acids, no proteins, no bacterial growth.",["MOA: bind 30S subunit → block aminoacyl-tRNA attachment to A site → bacteriostatic; actively transported into bacteria.","Spectrum: broad — Gram +, Gram −, rickettsiae, chlamydiae, mycoplasma, spirochaetes, Vibrio, Brucella, protozoa (Plasmodium, Entamoeba), Leptospira.","Uses (doxycycline drug of choice): rickettsial (scrub typhus, RMSF), chlamydial (trachoma, LGV, NGU), cholera, brucellosis (+ streptomycin/rifampicin), Lyme, leptospirosis (prophylaxis), atypical pneumonia, acne, malaria (prophylaxis, with quinine), plague, anthrax, H. pylori (tetracycline), MRSA skin; minocycline — acne, leprosy; demeclocycline — SIADH; tigecycline — MDR.","ADR: GI irritation, oesophageal ulcers (take upright with water), tooth discolouration and enamel hypoplasia, bone growth retardation (avoid < 8 yr, pregnancy, lactation), photosensitivity, hepatotoxicity (pregnancy), Fanconi syndrome (outdated), superinfection (candidiasis, C. difficile), pseudotumour cerebri, vestibular (minocycline); chelation with Ca, Mg, Al, Fe, milk."],"Tetracyclines + milk/antacids = no absorption; avoid in children & pregnancy (teeth)."],
"Tetracyclines are broad-spectrum antibiotics with a hydronaphthacene (four-ring) nucleus, obtained from Streptomyces. Classification: short-acting — tetracycline, oxytetracycline, chlortetracycline; intermediate — demeclocycline, methacycline; long-acting — doxycycline, minocycline; glycylcycline — tigecycline; newer — eravacycline, omadacycline, sarecycline. Mechanism: enter Gram-negative bacteria by passive diffusion through porins and active energy-dependent transport across the cytoplasmic membrane (absent in mammalian cells — selectivity); bind reversibly to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing attachment of aminoacyl-tRNA to the acceptor (A) site of the mRNA–ribosome complex — inhibit protein synthesis; bacteriostatic. Resistance: plasmid-mediated efflux pumps (tet genes), ribosomal protection proteins, enzymatic inactivation; cross-resistance within the class (tigecycline overcomes most). Spectrum: broad — Gram-positive (streptococci, pneumococci, staphylococci — resistance common, Bacillus anthracis, Listeria, Clostridia), Gram-negative (Neisseria, H. influenzae, Brucella, Vibrio cholerae, Yersinia pestis, Francisella, Helicobacter, Campylobacter, Bordetella; many Enterobacteriaceae resistant), anaerobes (some), Rickettsiae (all), Coxiella, Chlamydia, Mycoplasma, Ureaplasma, spirochaetes (Treponema, Borrelia, Leptospira), Actinomyces, Nocardia, Mycobacterium marinum, protozoa (Entamoeba histolytica, Balantidium, Plasmodium falciparum — slow); not Pseudomonas, Proteus. Pharmacokinetics: incomplete oral absorption of older tetracyclines (60–80%), reduced by food, milk and dairy products, antacids (Ca, Mg, Al), iron, zinc, sucralfate, bismuth (chelation) — doxycycline and minocycline nearly completely absorbed (95–100%) and less affected; widely distributed (bones, teeth — chelate calcium; liver, prostate; minocycline lipophilic — CSF, tears, saliva); cross placenta and into milk; tetracycline excreted in urine (avoid in renal failure); doxycycline excreted mainly in faeces (safe in renal failure), t½ 18–24 h (once/twice daily); minocycline t½ 16–20 h; enterohepatic circulation. Uses: doxycycline is now the preferred tetracycline — (1) rickettsial infections — scrub typhus, Indian tick typhus, Rocky Mountain spotted fever, epidemic and endemic typhus, Q fever (drug of choice, including children for short courses); (2) chlamydial infections — trachoma (azithromycin alternative), inclusion conjunctivitis, lymphogranuloma venereum, non-gonococcal urethritis, cervicitis, pelvic inflammatory disease (with ceftriaxone), psittacosis; (3) Mycoplasma and atypical pneumonia, community-acquired pneumonia, exacerbations of COPD; (4) cholera — single dose 300 mg (reduces stool volume and duration); (5) brucellosis (with rifampicin or streptomycin); (6) plague, tularaemia, anthrax (post-exposure prophylaxis and treatment); (7) Lyme disease (early), relapsing fever, leptospirosis (treatment and weekly prophylaxis 200 mg), syphilis (penicillin allergy); (8) acne vulgaris and rosacea (low dose; anti-inflammatory), hidradenitis; (9) malaria — chemoprophylaxis for travellers to chloroquine/mefloquine-resistant areas (100 mg daily), with quinine/artesunate in falciparum malaria; (10) H. pylori (tetracycline in bismuth quadruple therapy); (11) amoebiasis (luminal adjunct), balantidiasis; (12) MRSA skin infections (community), Vibrio vulnificus, Stenotrophomonas, actinomycosis, nocardiosis; (13) filariasis (Wolbachia — doxycycline kills endosymbiont), periodontitis (subantimicrobial doxycycline — MMP inhibition); minocycline — acne, leprosy (alternative in MDT), meningococcal carrier state, nocardiosis; demeclocycline — SIADH (nephrogenic DI effect); tigecycline (IV) — complicated skin and intra-abdominal infections by MDR organisms (MRSA, VRE, ESBL, Acinetobacter; increased mortality warning). Adverse effects: (1) GI — epigastric burning, nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea; oesophageal ulceration (pill oesophagitis — take with full glass of water, remain upright 30 min, not at bedtime); (2) superinfection — oral/vaginal candidiasis, antibiotic-associated diarrhoea, C. difficile colitis, staphylococcal enterocolitis; (3) teeth and bones — tetracycline–calcium orthophosphate complex deposited in developing teeth and bones causing permanent yellow-brown discolouration, enamel hypoplasia and temporary depression of bone growth — contraindicated in pregnancy (after 4 months), lactation and children < 8 years (except short courses of doxycycline for rickettsial disease); (4) hepatotoxicity — fatty liver, especially with high IV doses in pregnancy (acute fatty liver) and renal failure; (5) renal — aggravation of uraemia (anti-anabolic) in renal failure (except doxycycline), Fanconi syndrome with outdated/degraded tetracyclines (epitetracycline), nephrogenic diabetes insipidus (demeclocycline); (6) phototoxicity — photosensitivity, onycholysis (demeclocycline, doxycycline); (7) CNS — benign intracranial hypertension (pseudotumour cerebri) in infants and adults, especially with isotretinoin; vestibular toxicity — dizziness, vertigo, ataxia (minocycline); (8) hypersensitivity (rare), drug-induced lupus and skin/teeth pigmentation (minocycline); (9) thrombophlebitis (IV). Interactions: antacids, iron, calcium, zinc, milk reduce absorption; enzyme inducers (carbamazepine, phenytoin, rifampicin, barbiturates) reduce doxycycline levels; potentiate warfarin; may reduce oral contraceptive efficacy (debated); antagonise bactericidal penicillins; methoxyflurane (nephrotoxicity).",
[["Classification",[["Type","Drugs","Notes"],["Short-acting","Tetracycline, oxytetracycline","Renal excretion; chelation"],["Intermediate","Demeclocycline","SIADH"],["Long-acting","Doxycycline, minocycline","Better absorption; doxycycline safe in renal failure"],["Glycylcycline","Tigecycline","IV; MDR organisms"]]],
["Uses of doxycycline",["Rickettsial infections (scrub typhus) — drug of choice","Chlamydial infections, PID","Cholera (single dose)","Brucellosis, plague, anthrax, leptospirosis","Atypical pneumonia, COPD exacerbation","Acne, rosacea","Malaria prophylaxis/adjunct","Lyme disease, filariasis (Wolbachia)"]],
["Adverse effects",[["ADR","Note"],["GI irritation, oesophageal ulcers","Take upright with water"],["Teeth discolouration, bone growth","Avoid < 8 yr, pregnancy, lactation"],["Photosensitivity","Doxycycline, demeclocycline"],["Hepatotoxicity","Pregnancy, IV"],["Superinfection","Candida, C. difficile"],["Pseudotumour cerebri","With isotretinoin"],["Vestibular toxicity","Minocycline"],["Fanconi syndrome","Outdated tetracycline"]]]],
{i:"Tetracyclines are broad-spectrum bacteriostatic agents; doxycycline's better pharmacokinetics make it the preferred member.",f:"Tetracycline → enters via porins + active transport\n → binds 30S → blocks aminoacyl-tRNA at A site → ↓ protein synthesis (bacteriostatic)\n Chelation with Ca/Mg/Al/Fe → ↓ absorption; deposition in teeth and bones",c:"Tetracyclines, especially doxycycline, remain drugs of choice for rickettsial, chlamydial and several zoonotic infections, but must be avoided in young children and pregnancy and separated from chelating agents."});

Z("pharm10",9,"ph",["Chloramphenicol is a broad-spectrum bacteriostatic antibiotic that inhibits the 50S ribosomal peptidyl transferase; because of serious toxicity (aplastic anaemia, gray baby syndrome), it is reserved for specific situations like bacterial meningitis (in penicillin allergy), typhoid (if sensitive) and eye infections.","It blocks the peptide-joining machine on the ribosome; it also hits our mitochondrial ribosomes and marrow — hence its dangerous toxicity.",["MOA: binds 50S → inhibits peptidyl transferase → bacteriostatic (bactericidal for H. influenzae, N. meningitidis, S. pneumoniae).","Spectrum: Gram +, Gram −, anaerobes (B. fragilis), rickettsiae, chlamydiae, mycoplasma; Salmonella Typhi (resistance re-declining).","Uses: typhoid (sensitive strains), bacterial meningitis (penicillin allergy/resource-poor), brain abscess, anaerobic infections, rickettsial (when doxycycline contraindicated), eye infections (topical drops), plague.","ADR: dose-related reversible marrow suppression; idiosyncratic irreversible aplastic anaemia (1 in 25,000–40,000; even after eye drops); gray baby syndrome (neonates — deficient glucuronidation: vomiting, cyanosis, ashen colour, hypothermia, shock); optic neuritis, GI upset, superinfection; enzyme inhibitor (↑ phenytoin, warfarin, sulfonylureas)."],"Chloramphenicol: 'Gray baby + aplastic anaemia'."],
"Chloramphenicol, originally isolated from Streptomyces venezuelae (1947), is now produced synthetically; a nitrobenzene derivative with a dichloroacetamide side chain. Mechanism: binds reversibly to the 50S ribosomal subunit (near the binding site of macrolides and clindamycin — mutual antagonism) and inhibits peptidyl transferase, preventing peptide bond formation — inhibition of protein synthesis; primarily bacteriostatic but bactericidal against H. influenzae, N. meningitidis and S. pneumoniae at clinical concentrations. It also inhibits mitochondrial protein synthesis in mammalian cells (especially erythroid precursors) — basis of dose-related marrow toxicity. Resistance: plasmid-mediated chloramphenicol acetyltransferase (inactivation), reduced permeability, efflux. Spectrum: broad — Gram-positive (S. pneumoniae, S. pyogenes, staphylococci), Gram-negative (H. influenzae, Neisseria, Salmonella, Shigella, E. coli, Klebsiella, Brucella, Bordetella, Vibrio), anaerobes (including Bacteroides fragilis, Clostridium), Rickettsiae, Chlamydia, Mycoplasma, spirochaetes; not Pseudomonas. Pharmacokinetics: rapidly and completely absorbed orally; palmitate ester (oral suspension) and succinate ester (IV) are prodrugs; widely distributed — excellent penetration into CSF (30–50% of plasma levels even without inflammation), brain, eye, milk, crosses placenta; 50% protein bound; metabolised by glucuronide conjugation in liver (deficient in neonates); t½ 3–5 h; excreted in urine (inactive conjugates). Dose: 50–100 mg/kg/day in 4 divided doses (adult 500 mg–1 g 6-hourly). Uses (restricted because of toxicity): (1) enteric fever — when strains are sensitive (susceptibility has returned in many areas); cheap and effective orally; (2) bacterial meningitis — in patients with serious β-lactam allergy, resource-limited settings (pneumococcal, meningococcal, H. influenzae); (3) brain abscess (CNS penetration and anaerobic coverage, with penicillin); (4) anaerobic infections — intra-abdominal, pelvic (alternatives preferred); (5) rickettsial infections (in pregnancy or children when tetracyclines contraindicated), plague, tularaemia, melioidosis; (6) topical — bacterial conjunctivitis (eye drops/ointment — cheap, broad), ear infections; (7) VRE (in some cases). Adverse effects: (1) bone marrow depression — (a) dose-related, predictable, reversible suppression of erythropoiesis (reticulocytopenia, anaemia, vacuolated erythroid precursors, raised serum iron), sometimes leucopenia and thrombocytopenia, with high doses (> 4 g/day) or prolonged therapy; (b) idiosyncratic, non-dose-related, irreversible aplastic anaemia (1 in 25,000–40,000 courses), often fatal, may occur weeks to months after therapy, even after topical use; associated with later leukaemia; monitor blood counts; (2) gray baby syndrome — in neonates (especially premature) given high doses, due to inadequate glucuronyl transferase and immature renal excretion causing drug accumulation; features within 2–9 days — vomiting, refusal to feed, abdominal distension, irregular respiration, hypothermia, flaccidity, ashen-gray cyanosis, cardiovascular collapse, death (40%); mitochondrial dysfunction; avoid in neonates or use reduced doses with monitoring; (3) hypersensitivity — rashes, fever, angioedema; Jarisch–Herxheimer reactions; (4) GI — nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, glossitis, superinfection (candidiasis); (5) optic neuritis, peripheral neuritis (prolonged therapy); (6) haemolysis in G6PD deficiency. Interactions: inhibits CYP2C9 and 3A4 — prolongs t½ and increases toxicity of phenytoin, tolbutamide, chlorpropamide, warfarin, cyclosporine; enzyme inducers (phenobarbitone, rifampicin) reduce its levels; antagonises bactericidal action of penicillins and aminoglycosides; mutual antagonism with macrolides and clindamycin at 50S site.",
[["Uses",["Typhoid (sensitive strains)","Bacterial meningitis (β-lactam allergy)","Brain abscess","Anaerobic infections","Rickettsial infections in pregnancy/children","Conjunctivitis (eye drops)"]],
["Adverse effects",[["ADR","Feature"],["Dose-related marrow suppression","Reversible; erythroid"],["Aplastic anaemia","Idiosyncratic, irreversible, often fatal"],["Gray baby syndrome","Neonates; ↓ glucuronidation"],["Optic/peripheral neuritis","Prolonged use"],["Hypersensitivity, GI upset, superinfection","—"]]],
["Gray baby syndrome",["Cause: deficient glucuronidation + immature renal excretion","Features: vomiting, distension, hypothermia, ashen cyanosis, shock","Prevention: avoid in neonates or reduce dose with levels"]]],
{c:"Chloramphenicol is broad spectrum with excellent CNS penetration, but its potential to cause aplastic anaemia and gray baby syndrome restricts it to situations where safer alternatives are unsuitable."});

Z("pharm10",10,"ph",["Aminoglycosides (gentamicin, amikacin, streptomycin, tobramycin, neomycin) are bactericidal antibiotics that bind the 30S ribosome, mainly against aerobic Gram-negative bacteria; they are given by injection and can damage the ear and kidney.","They make the ribosome misread the genetic message, producing faulty proteins that poke holes in the bacterial membrane — rapid killing that depends on peak concentration.",["MOA: enter via O2-dependent transport (no activity against anaerobes), bind 30S → misreading of mRNA, block initiation, polysome breakup → bactericidal, concentration-dependent, post-antibiotic effect (once-daily dosing).","Spectrum: aerobic Gram-negative bacilli (E. coli, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas — gentamicin, tobramycin, amikacin), staphylococci; synergy with β-lactams for enterococci, streptococci; streptomycin/amikacin — M. tuberculosis; streptomycin — plague, tularaemia.","Uses: serious Gram-negative sepsis, complicated UTI, Pseudomonas (with antipseudomonal β-lactam), endocarditis (with penicillin), TB (streptomycin, amikacin), plague, tularaemia, neomycin (oral — hepatic encephalopathy, bowel prep; topical), febrile neutropenia.","ADR: ototoxicity — vestibular (streptomycin, gentamicin) and cochlear (amikacin, neomycin, kanamycin), irreversible, ↑ with loop diuretics; nephrotoxicity (proximal tubule, reversible; neomycin > gentamicin > tobramycin > amikacin > streptomycin); neuromuscular blockade (apnoea; reversed by calcium, neostigmine); avoid in pregnancy (fetal deafness), myasthenia gravis; TDM of trough levels."],"Aminoglycoside toxicity: 'ear, kidney, neuromuscular junction'."],
"Aminoglycosides are polycationic, water-soluble amino sugars linked glycosidically to an aminocyclitol ring. Drugs: systemic — streptomycin, gentamicin, kanamycin, tobramycin, amikacin, sisomicin, netilmicin, plazomicin; topical/oral — neomycin, framycetin, paromomycin. Common properties: polar (poorly absorbed orally, poor CNS/eye penetration, not metabolised), excreted unchanged by glomerular filtration (accumulate in renal failure), active at alkaline pH (less in acidic, anaerobic abscesses and purulent sites), bactericidal (concentration-dependent killing, prolonged post-antibiotic effect), narrow therapeutic index, ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity, neuromuscular blockade, primarily aerobic Gram-negative spectrum, synergistic with β-lactams, partial cross-resistance. Mechanism: diffuse through porin channels of the outer membrane, then actively transported across the cytoplasmic membrane by an oxygen-dependent process (hence inactive against anaerobes; enhanced by cell wall–active drugs — synergy); bind to 30S ribosomal subunit (16S rRNA) causing (1) blockade of initiation complex formation, (2) misreading of mRNA — incorporation of wrong amino acids — production of aberrant proteins inserted into the membrane, increasing permeability and further drug entry, (3) break-up of polysomes into non-functional monosomes; rapid bactericidal effect. Resistance: inactivating enzymes (acetyltransferases, phosphotransferases, adenyltransferases — plasmid-mediated; amikacin resists many), reduced uptake, altered ribosomal binding (streptomycin), 16S rRNA methylases (pan-resistance). Spectrum: aerobic Gram-negative bacilli — E. coli, Klebsiella, Enterobacter, Proteus, Serratia, Pseudomonas aeruginosa (tobramycin most active, gentamicin, amikacin), Acinetobacter (amikacin), Yersinia pestis, Francisella tularensis, Brucella; Staphylococcus aureus; Mycobacterium tuberculosis (streptomycin, amikacin, kanamycin), M. avium (amikacin), Nocardia (amikacin); synergy against enterococci, viridans streptococci, Listeria with penicillin/ampicillin; not anaerobes, streptococci alone, pneumococci. Pharmacokinetics: IM/IV; Vd ≈ extracellular fluid; t½ 2–3 h (prolonged in renal failure, newborns); concentrated in renal cortex and inner ear endolymph/perilymph (toxicity); cross placenta. Dosing: once-daily extended-interval dosing (gentamicin 5–7 mg/kg/day; amikacin 15–20 mg/kg/day) exploits concentration-dependent killing and PAE and reduces toxicity (saturable uptake); therapeutic drug monitoring — peak and trough levels (gentamicin trough < 1 µg/mL); dose adjustment by creatinine clearance. Uses: (1) serious Gram-negative infections — septicaemia, nosocomial pneumonia, complicated UTI and pyelonephritis, peritonitis, febrile neutropenia — usually combined with a β-lactam (empirical cover, synergy, prevention of resistance); (2) Pseudomonas infections — with antipseudomonal penicillin/cephalosporin; cystic fibrosis (inhaled tobramycin, amikacin); (3) infective endocarditis — enterococcal (ampicillin + gentamicin), viridans streptococcal (penicillin + gentamicin, short course), staphylococcal prosthetic valve; (4) tuberculosis — streptomycin (first-line injectable historically), amikacin, kanamycin (MDR-TB; now largely replaced by oral regimens); (5) plague and tularaemia (streptomycin, gentamicin — drugs of choice), brucellosis (with doxycycline); (6) neonatal sepsis (ampicillin + gentamicin); (7) listeriosis (with ampicillin); (8) topical — neomycin/framycetin in skin, eye and ear infections; gentamicin eye drops, gentamicin–impregnated beads in osteomyelitis; (9) oral neomycin/paromomycin — hepatic encephalopathy (reduce ammonia-producing gut flora), preoperative bowel preparation, paromomycin for amoebiasis (luminal), cryptosporidiosis, leishmaniasis (IM); (10) Mycobacterium avium complex, Nocardia (amikacin). Adverse effects: (1) ototoxicity — accumulation in perilymph and endolymph damages sensory hair cells; vestibular toxicity (streptomycin, gentamicin — vertigo, ataxia, nystagmus, loss of balance) and cochlear toxicity (amikacin, kanamycin, neomycin — tinnitus, high-frequency hearing loss, deafness); usually irreversible; risk factors — high trough levels, prolonged therapy (> 5–7 days), renal impairment, elderly, loop diuretics (furosemide, ethacrynic acid), vancomycin, genetic susceptibility (mitochondrial 12S rRNA A1555G mutation); fetal eighth nerve damage — avoid in pregnancy; (2) nephrotoxicity — proximal tubular cell damage (accumulation in lysosomes), non-oliguric acute kidney injury with rising creatinine, usually reversible; risk increased by prolonged use, high troughs, elderly, dehydration, pre-existing renal disease, concomitant nephrotoxins (amphotericin B, vancomycin, cisplatin, cyclosporine, NSAIDs, contrast, loop diuretics); order of nephrotoxicity — neomycin > gentamicin > tobramycin > amikacin > streptomycin; (3) neuromuscular blockade — inhibit presynaptic ACh release (Ca²⁺ antagonism) and reduce postsynaptic sensitivity; respiratory paralysis especially after rapid IV injection, intraperitoneal/intrapleural instillation, in myasthenia gravis, with neuromuscular blockers or hypocalcaemia; reversed by IV calcium gluconate and neostigmine; (4) others — hypersensitivity (rare; streptomycin dermatitis in handlers), peripheral neuritis, optic neuritis (streptomycin), hypomagnesaemia, malabsorption and superinfection (oral neomycin). Precautions/contraindications: pregnancy, renal impairment (dose adjust), elderly, myasthenia gravis, pre-existing hearing loss, concomitant ototoxic/nephrotoxic drugs; avoid mixing with β-lactams in same infusion (inactivation).",
[["Common properties",["Polar: poor oral absorption, poor CNS penetration","Renal excretion unchanged","Bactericidal, concentration-dependent, PAE","Inactive against anaerobes (O2-dependent uptake)","Synergy with β-lactams","Ototoxicity, nephrotoxicity, NM blockade","Narrow therapeutic index (TDM)"]],
["Drugs and uses",[["Drug","Main use"],["Streptomycin","TB, plague, tularaemia, brucellosis"],["Gentamicin","Gram-negative sepsis, endocarditis synergy, neonatal sepsis"],["Tobramycin","Pseudomonas (CF inhaled)"],["Amikacin","Resistant Gram −, MDR-TB, MAC, Nocardia"],["Neomycin","Hepatic encephalopathy, bowel prep, topical"],["Paromomycin","Amoebiasis, cryptosporidiosis, leishmaniasis"]]],
["Adverse effects",[["Toxicity","Features","Risk factors"],["Vestibular","Vertigo, ataxia (streptomycin, gentamicin)","High troughs, prolonged use"],["Cochlear","Tinnitus, deafness (amikacin, neomycin)","Loop diuretics, elderly, A1555G"],["Nephrotoxicity","Proximal tubule, reversible AKI","Other nephrotoxins, dehydration"],["NM blockade","Apnoea","Myasthenia, NM blockers; reverse with Ca²⁺, neostigmine"],["Fetal","Eighth nerve damage","Pregnancy"]]]],
{i:"Aminoglycosides are potent bactericidal agents against aerobic Gram-negative bacteria whose use is limited by dose-related ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity.",f:"Aminoglycoside → porins → O2-dependent active transport (↑ by β-lactams)\n → binds 30S → blocks initiation, mRNA misreading, polysome breakup\n → faulty membrane proteins → ↑ uptake → rapid killing\n Toxicity: accumulates in renal cortex and inner ear",c:"Aminoglycosides like gentamicin and amikacin remain important for serious Gram-negative infections and synergy; once-daily dosing, drug monitoring and avoidance of other ototoxic/nephrotoxic drugs minimise their toxicity."});

Z("pharm10",11,"ph",["Erythromycin is a macrolide antibiotic that binds the 50S ribosome to inhibit bacterial protein synthesis; it is mainly active against Gram-positive organisms and atypical bacteria and is a common alternative in penicillin-allergic patients.","Erythromycin blocks the ribosome's exit tunnel so the growing protein chain cannot move on (translocation).",["MOA: binds 23S rRNA of 50S → blocks translocation → bacteriostatic (cidal at high conc.); resistance — methylation (erm), efflux (mef).","Spectrum: Gram + (streptococci, pneumococci, staph — resistance), Corynebacterium diphtheriae, Bordetella pertussis, Mycoplasma, Chlamydia, Legionella, Campylobacter, Treponema, H. ducreyi.","Uses: penicillin alternative (streptococcal pharyngitis, rheumatic fever prophylaxis, syphilis, tetanus), atypical pneumonia, pertussis (drug of choice — reduces spread), diphtheria (carriers), Campylobacter enteritis, chancroid, acne, chlamydia in pregnancy, ophthalmia neonatorum prophylaxis (ointment), prokinetic (gastroparesis — motilin agonist).","ADR: GI upset (motilin agonism — most common), cholestatic jaundice (estolate), QT prolongation/torsades, ototoxicity (high dose), infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis; CYP3A4 inhibitor — ↑ theophylline, carbamazepine, warfarin, statins, terfenadine/cisapride (fatal arrhythmias), cyclosporine, digoxin."],"Erythromycin = CYP3A4 inhibitor + motilin agonist (GI upset)."],
"Macrolides are antibiotics with a large macrocyclic lactone ring: 14-membered — erythromycin, clarithromycin, roxithromycin; 15-membered (azalide) — azithromycin; 16-membered — spiramycin; ketolide — telithromycin; fidaxomicin (18-membered, C. difficile). Erythromycin was isolated from Streptomyces erythreus (1952). Mechanism: binds reversibly to the 23S rRNA of the 50S ribosomal subunit near the peptidyl transferase centre, blocking the polypeptide exit tunnel and inhibiting translocation of peptidyl-tRNA from the A site to the P site; nascent peptide chain dissociates — inhibition of protein synthesis; bacteriostatic (bactericidal at high concentrations for sensitive organisms); competes with chloramphenicol and clindamycin for binding (antagonism). Resistance: target-site modification by erm-encoded methylases (MLSB resistance — cross-resistance to macrolides, lincosamides, streptogramin B), active efflux (mef genes), ribosomal mutations, esterases; increasing in pneumococci and streptococci. Spectrum: narrow, similar to penicillin plus atypicals — Gram-positive cocci (S. pyogenes, S. pneumoniae, viridans streptococci, S. aureus — penicillinase producers but not MRSA), Gram-positive bacilli (Corynebacterium diphtheriae, Clostridium, Listeria, Bacillus anthracis), Gram-negative — Neisseria, Bordetella pertussis, Campylobacter jejuni, H. ducreyi, Legionella pneumophila, Moraxella, H. influenzae (weak); atypicals — Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Chlamydia trachomatis and pneumoniae, Ureaplasma; Treponema pallidum; Rickettsiae (some); not Enterobacteriaceae, Pseudomonas. Pharmacokinetics: erythromycin base is acid-labile (enteric-coated); salts/esters — stearate, ethylsuccinate, estolate (better absorbed but hepatotoxic); lactobionate and gluceptate IV; incomplete oral absorption, food reduces absorption of base; widely distributed (except CSF, brain), enters macrophages and PMNs, crosses placenta, milk; concentrated in liver and bile; metabolised by CYP3A4 (and inhibits it — forms inactive complex), t½ 1.5 h (dosing 6-hourly). Uses: (1) as a substitute for penicillin in allergic patients — streptococcal pharyngitis, tonsillitis, scarlet fever, erysipelas, cellulitis, prophylaxis of rheumatic fever and bacterial endocarditis, pneumococcal pneumonia, diphtheria, tetanus, syphilis (not preferred), leptospirosis; (2) atypical pneumonia — Mycoplasma, Chlamydophila, Legionella (newer macrolides preferred); (3) whooping cough (pertussis) — drug of choice; eradicates organism and reduces infectivity; prophylaxis of contacts (azithromycin in infants); (4) diphtheria — eliminates carrier state (with antitoxin in cases); (5) Campylobacter enteritis (azithromycin preferred); (6) chlamydial infections — urethritis, cervicitis, conjunctivitis and pneumonia in infants, in pregnancy; ophthalmia neonatorum prophylaxis (0.5% ointment); chancroid, lymphogranuloma venereum (alternative); (7) acne vulgaris (topical/oral), erythrasma; (8) gastroparesis and before endoscopy in GI bleeding (motilin agonist; low dose IV); (9) Lyme disease, bacillary angiomatosis (alternative). Adverse effects: (1) GI — epigastric pain, cramps, nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea (most common; motilin receptor agonist — stimulates GI motility), limits compliance; (2) hepatotoxicity — cholestatic hepatitis with jaundice, fever, eosinophilia, mainly with estolate ester after 10–20 days (hypersensitivity); avoid estolate in pregnancy and liver disease; (3) QT prolongation and torsades de pointes (especially IV, with other QT drugs or CYP3A4 inhibitors); (4) reversible sensorineural hearing loss and tinnitus at high doses (IV), elderly, renal failure; (5) hypersensitivity — rashes, fever; (6) infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis when given to neonates < 2 weeks; (7) thrombophlebitis (IV), superinfection. Drug interactions (CYP3A4 and P-gp inhibition): raises levels of theophylline (toxicity, seizures), carbamazepine, warfarin (bleeding), cyclosporine, tacrolimus, digoxin (gut flora and P-gp), ergotamine (ergotism), statins — simvastatin, lovastatin (rhabdomyolysis), midazolam, triazolam, disopyramide, quinidine, and particularly terfenadine, astemizole, cisapride, pimozide (QT prolongation — fatal ventricular arrhythmias; contraindicated); colchicine toxicity.",
[["Macrolide comparison",[["Feature","Erythromycin","Clarithromycin","Azithromycin"],["Acid stability","Labile","Stable","Stable"],["t½","1.5 h","3–7 h","40–68 h"],["Dosing","6-hourly","12-hourly","Once daily, 3–5 days"],["H. influenzae","Weak","Good","Best"],["CYP3A4 inhibition","Strong","Strong","Minimal"],["GI upset","Marked","Less","Least"],["Special","Prokinetic","H. pylori, MAC","STIs, typhoid, trachoma"]]],
["Uses of erythromycin",["Penicillin allergy: streptococcal infections, rheumatic fever prophylaxis","Pertussis (drug of choice)","Diphtheria carriers","Atypical pneumonia","Campylobacter, chlamydia (pregnancy)","Ophthalmia neonatorum prophylaxis","Gastroparesis (prokinetic)"]],
["Adverse effects and interactions",["GI upset (motilin agonism)","Cholestatic jaundice (estolate)","QT prolongation","Ototoxicity (high dose)","Pyloric stenosis in neonates","CYP3A4 inhibition: theophylline, carbamazepine, warfarin, statins, terfenadine/cisapride"]]],
{c:"Erythromycin is a useful penicillin alternative with activity against atypical organisms and is the drug of choice for pertussis; GI intolerance, QT prolongation and CYP3A4 interactions have led to wider use of newer macrolides."});

Z("pharm10",12,"ph",["Clarithromycin is a semisynthetic macrolide with better acid stability, absorption, tolerability and wider spectrum than erythromycin; it is a key component of H. pylori eradication therapy and treatment of Mycobacterium avium complex.","Clarithromycin is an upgraded erythromycin — stays intact in stomach acid, lasts longer, and also hits H. pylori and atypical mycobacteria.",["MOA: binds 50S (23S rRNA) like erythromycin; active metabolite 14-hydroxy-clarithromycin (synergy against H. influenzae).","Spectrum: erythromycin spectrum + more H. influenzae, Moraxella, Legionella, Chlamydia, Mycoplasma, H. pylori, M. avium complex, M. leprae, Toxoplasma.","Uses: H. pylori triple therapy, MAC treatment and prophylaxis in AIDS (with ethambutol), community-acquired pneumonia, sinusitis, otitis, pharyngitis, skin infections, leprosy (alternative), pertussis.","ADR: GI upset (less), metallic taste, headache, QT prolongation, hepatotoxicity; potent CYP3A4 inhibitor — interactions (statins, carbamazepine, colchicine, warfarin); teratogenic in animals (avoid pregnancy); twice daily dosing."],"Clarithromycin: 'H. pylori + MAC'."],
"Clarithromycin is a 14-membered semisynthetic macrolide (6-O-methylerythromycin). Advantages over erythromycin: acid stable (better oral bioavailability ~55%, not affected by food), longer t½ (3–7 h; twice-daily dosing; extended-release once daily), higher tissue and intracellular concentrations, active 14-hydroxy metabolite (additive/synergistic against H. influenzae), broader spectrum and less GI intolerance. Mechanism: binds 23S rRNA in 50S ribosomal subunit and inhibits translocation (as erythromycin); bacteriostatic. Spectrum: Gram-positive cocci (streptococci, pneumococci, MSSA; more potent than erythromycin), H. influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis, Legionella, Chlamydophila pneumoniae, C. trachomatis, Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Bordetella pertussis, Helicobacter pylori (most active macrolide), Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare complex, M. leprae, M. chelonae, M. kansasii, Toxoplasma gondii, Borrelia burgdorferi. Pharmacokinetics: metabolised in liver by CYP3A4 (saturable — non-linear kinetics), renal excretion of parent and metabolite (dose reduction if CrCl < 30). Uses: (1) Helicobacter pylori eradication — clarithromycin 500 mg BD with PPI and amoxicillin/metronidazole (triple therapy) for 14 days (resistance rising, limiting use where > 15%); (2) Mycobacterium avium complex — treatment (with ethambutol ± rifabutin) and prophylaxis in AIDS (CD4 < 50; azithromycin alternative), pulmonary MAC disease; (3) respiratory tract infections — community-acquired pneumonia (atypical coverage), acute bacterial sinusitis, exacerbations of chronic bronchitis, streptococcal pharyngitis in penicillin allergy, otitis media; (4) skin and soft tissue infections; (5) leprosy (alternative in MDT, especially rifampicin-resistant), Lyme disease, pertussis, toxoplasmosis (with pyrimethamine), cryptosporidiosis; (6) Mycobacterium ulcerans (Buruli ulcer, with rifampicin). Adverse effects: GI — nausea, diarrhoea, abdominal pain, dysgeusia (metallic/bitter taste — common), dyspepsia; headache, insomnia, nightmares, psychosis; hepatotoxicity (cholestatic hepatitis), QT prolongation and torsades, reversible ototoxicity, hypoglycaemia with sulfonylureas, C. difficile colitis; increased long-term cardiovascular mortality reported in patients with coronary disease (caution); teratogenic in animal studies — avoid in pregnancy unless no alternative. Drug interactions: potent CYP3A4 and P-gp inhibitor — raises levels of simvastatin, lovastatin, atorvastatin (myopathy), carbamazepine, theophylline, warfarin, digoxin, cyclosporine, tacrolimus, colchicine (fatal toxicity), midazolam, ergot alkaloids, pimozide, sildenafil, rifabutin (uveitis), calcium channel blockers (hypotension), quetiapine; rifampicin and efavirenz reduce clarithromycin levels; contraindicated with cisapride, pimozide, ergotamine, simvastatin/lovastatin, ticagrelor, colchicine in renal/hepatic impairment.",
[["Advantages over erythromycin",["Acid stable, better absorption","Longer t½ (BD dosing)","Active 14-OH metabolite","Broader spectrum (H. pylori, MAC, H. influenzae)","Less GI upset"]],
["Uses",["H. pylori triple therapy","MAC treatment and prophylaxis","CAP, sinusitis, otitis, pharyngitis","Skin infections","Leprosy (alternative), toxoplasmosis, Lyme","Pertussis"]],
["Adverse effects and interactions",["Dysgeusia, GI upset","QT prolongation","Hepatotoxicity","Avoid in pregnancy","CYP3A4 inhibitor: statins, colchicine, carbamazepine, warfarin, rifabutin"]]],
{c:"Clarithromycin improves on erythromycin in stability, pharmacokinetics and spectrum; it is central to H. pylori and MAC therapy, but its CYP3A4 inhibition demands careful review of co-medications."});

Z("pharm10",13,"ph",["Clindamycin is a lincosamide antibiotic that binds the 50S ribosome; it is active against Gram-positive cocci (including many MRSA) and anaerobes, but is notorious for causing Clostridioides difficile (pseudomembranous) colitis.","Clindamycin is the 'anaerobe + staph' specialist; it also switches off toxin production by streptococci and staphylococci.",["MOA: binds 50S (23S rRNA) at same site as macrolides/chloramphenicol → inhibits peptide translocation; bacteriostatic; suppresses toxin synthesis (TSST-1, PVL, streptococcal toxins).","Spectrum: Gram + cocci (staph incl. community MRSA, streptococci), anaerobes (B. fragilis, Clostridium perfringens, Peptostreptococcus, Fusobacterium), Toxoplasma, Pneumocystis (with primaquine), Plasmodium; inducible resistance (D-test).","Uses: anaerobic infections (aspiration pneumonia, lung abscess, dental infections), skin and soft tissue/bone infections (MRSA), necrotising fasciitis and toxic shock (with penicillin — toxin suppression), bacterial vaginosis, acne (topical), PCP and toxoplasmosis (alternatives), malaria (with quinine), endocarditis prophylaxis (penicillin allergy).","ADR: diarrhoea, C. difficile colitis (pseudomembranous — treat with oral vancomycin/fidaxomicin), rash, hepatotoxicity, NM blockade (potentiation), metallic taste; excellent bone penetration, poor CSF."],"Clindamycin: 'Anaerobes above the diaphragm' + C. difficile risk."],
"Clindamycin is a semisynthetic chlorinated derivative of lincomycin (lincosamide). Mechanism: binds to the 50S ribosomal subunit (23S rRNA) at a site overlapping macrolides and chloramphenicol, inhibiting peptide bond formation and translocation — protein synthesis inhibition; bacteriostatic (bactericidal at high concentrations for some strains); importantly suppresses synthesis of bacterial exotoxins and virulence factors (TSST-1, Panton–Valentine leukocidin, streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins, M protein), useful in toxin-mediated disease. Resistance: target methylation (erm — MLSB phenotype; inducible resistance detected by D-zone test in erythromycin-resistant staphylococci), efflux, enzymatic inactivation. Spectrum: Gram-positive aerobes — Staphylococcus aureus (MSSA and many community-acquired MRSA), S. epidermidis, Streptococcus pyogenes, viridans streptococci, pneumococci; anaerobes — Bacteroides fragilis (increasing resistance), Prevotella, Fusobacterium, Peptostreptococcus, Clostridium perfringens (not C. difficile), Actinomyces; protozoa — Toxoplasma gondii, Plasmodium falciparum, Babesia; Pneumocystis jirovecii (with primaquine); Gardnerella; not enterococci, aerobic Gram-negative bacilli, Mycoplasma. Pharmacokinetics: oral bioavailability ~90% (unaffected by food), IV and IM (phosphate), topical (gel, lotion) and vaginal cream; widely distributed including bone, abscesses, synovial fluid, intracellular (macrophages); poor CSF penetration; metabolised in liver (CYP3A4), excreted in bile and urine; t½ 2–3 h. Uses: (1) anaerobic infections — aspiration pneumonia, lung abscess, empyema (anaerobes 'above the diaphragm'), odontogenic and dental infections, peritonsillar abscess, Ludwig angina; intra-abdominal and pelvic infections (with Gram-negative cover; increasing B. fragilis resistance limits use); (2) staphylococcal and streptococcal infections — skin and soft tissue infections including community MRSA, cellulitis, abscesses, osteomyelitis and septic arthritis (excellent bone penetration), diabetic foot infections; (3) toxin-mediated infections — necrotising fasciitis, streptococcal and staphylococcal toxic shock syndrome, gas gangrene (with penicillin/β-lactam — 'Eagle effect' and toxin suppression); (4) bacterial vaginosis (oral/vaginal), pelvic inflammatory disease (with gentamicin); (5) acne vulgaris (topical, often with benzoyl peroxide to reduce resistance), rosacea, hidradenitis; (6) Pneumocystis pneumonia (clindamycin + primaquine — mild–moderate in sulfa allergy), toxoplasmic encephalitis (clindamycin + pyrimethamine in sulfa allergy), ocular toxoplasmosis; (7) malaria — severe falciparum malaria with quinine or artesunate (especially in pregnancy and children where doxycycline contraindicated); babesiosis (with quinine); (8) prophylaxis — infective endocarditis before dental procedures in penicillin allergy (now discouraged due to C. difficile; azithromycin preferred), surgical prophylaxis in β-lactam allergy; (9) anthrax (with ciprofloxacin — toxin suppression), actinomycosis. Adverse effects: diarrhoea (up to 20%); Clostridioides difficile–associated diarrhoea and pseudomembranous colitis (one of the highest-risk antibiotics; may occur weeks after stopping) — stop clindamycin, oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin (metronidazole for mild cases historically); nausea, vomiting, metallic taste (IV); rashes, Stevens–Johnson syndrome (rare), DRESS; reversible elevation of liver enzymes, jaundice; neutropenia, thrombocytopenia; thrombophlebitis; neuromuscular blockade potentiation (caution with NM blockers); rapid IV bolus may cause cardiac arrest (infuse slowly). Interactions: antagonism with erythromycin and chloramphenicol (same site); potentiates neuromuscular blockers; CYP3A4 inducers/inhibitors alter levels.",
[["Spectrum",[["Covered","Not covered"],["Staphylococci (incl. many CA-MRSA)","Enterococci"],["Streptococci","Aerobic Gram-negative bacilli"],["Anaerobes (Bacteroides, Clostridium perfringens, Fusobacterium)","C. difficile"],["Toxoplasma, Pneumocystis, Plasmodium","Mycoplasma"]]],
["Uses",["Anaerobic infections: aspiration pneumonia, lung/dental abscess","Skin, soft tissue, bone/joint infections (MRSA)","Toxic shock, necrotising fasciitis (toxin suppression)","Bacterial vaginosis, PID","Acne (topical)","PCP (+ primaquine), toxoplasmosis (+ pyrimethamine)","Malaria (+ quinine/artesunate)"]],
["Adverse effects",["Diarrhoea","C. difficile pseudomembranous colitis","Rash, SJS (rare)","Hepatotoxicity","NM blockade potentiation","Metallic taste, thrombophlebitis"]]],
{c:"Clindamycin is valuable for Gram-positive and anaerobic infections and for suppressing bacterial toxins, but its strong association with C. difficile colitis requires judicious use."});

Z("pharm10",14,"ph",["Vancomycin is a glycopeptide antibiotic that inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by binding D-Ala-D-Ala; it is the mainstay against MRSA and other resistant Gram-positive bacteria, and orally treats C. difficile colitis.","Vancomycin caps the end of the cell-wall building block (D-Ala-D-Ala) so it cannot be added — a big molecule that cannot enter Gram-negative bacteria.",["MOA: binds D-alanyl-D-alanine terminus of peptidoglycan precursors → blocks transglycosylation and transpeptidation → bactericidal (slow, time-dependent); VRE — D-Ala-D-Lac (vanA).","Spectrum: Gram + only — MRSA, MRSE, penicillin-resistant pneumococci, enterococci (bacteriostatic), Clostridioides difficile, Corynebacterium jeikeium; not Gram − (too large for porins).","Uses: MRSA infections (bacteraemia, endocarditis, pneumonia, osteomyelitis), empirical in meningitis (with ceftriaxone), febrile neutropenia with line infection, prosthetic infections, enterococcal endocarditis (penicillin allergy), oral for C. difficile colitis (not absorbed), surgical prophylaxis (MRSA carriers).","ADR: red man (red neck) syndrome — histamine release with rapid infusion (infuse over ≥ 60 min, antihistamine), nephrotoxicity (↑ with aminoglycosides, piperacillin–tazobactam), ototoxicity, thrombophlebitis, neutropenia, DRESS; TDM — AUC/MIC 400–600 or trough 15–20 µg/mL; not absorbed orally."],"Vancomycin rapid infusion → 'red man syndrome'."],
"Vancomycin is a tricyclic glycopeptide antibiotic obtained from Streptomyces (Amycolatopsis) orientalis; teicoplanin, telavancin, dalbavancin and oritavancin are related glycopeptides/lipoglycopeptides. Mechanism: binds with high affinity to the D-alanyl-D-alanine terminus of the pentapeptide side chain of peptidoglycan precursors (lipid II) at the outer surface of the cytoplasmic membrane; this steric hindrance blocks transglycosylation (polymerisation of glycan chains) and transpeptidation (cross-linking), inhibiting cell wall synthesis at a step earlier than β-lactams; bactericidal for dividing Gram-positive organisms (except enterococci — bacteriostatic; synergy with gentamicin); time-dependent (AUC/MIC). Large molecule cannot penetrate the outer membrane of Gram-negative bacteria. Resistance: vancomycin-resistant enterococci (VRE) — van genes (vanA, vanB) alter the terminus to D-Ala-D-lactate (1000-fold lower affinity) or D-Ala-D-Ser (vanC); vancomycin-intermediate S. aureus (VISA — thickened cell wall trapping drug) and vancomycin-resistant S. aureus (VRSA — vanA acquired from enterococci) are rare. Spectrum: Gram-positive bacteria only — Staphylococcus aureus including methicillin-resistant (MRSA), coagulase-negative staphylococci (MRSE), Streptococcus pyogenes, S. pneumoniae (including penicillin-resistant), viridans streptococci, Enterococcus faecalis and faecium (non-VRE), Clostridioides difficile, Clostridium spp, Corynebacterium (including C. jeikeium), Listeria, Bacillus. Pharmacokinetics: not absorbed orally (oral therapy acts locally in colon — C. difficile), given IV by slow infusion (IM painful); widely distributed (pleural, pericardial, synovial, ascitic fluids, bone), CSF penetration only with inflamed meninges; 90% excreted unchanged by glomerular filtration — t½ 6 h (up to 7 days in anuria; not removed by standard haemodialysis); dose adjustment by renal function; therapeutic drug monitoring — AUC-guided dosing (AUC/MIC 400–600) preferred to trough levels (15–20 µg/mL for serious infections previously). Dose: 15–20 mg/kg IV every 8–12 h (loading 25–30 mg/kg in critically ill); oral 125 mg 6-hourly for 10 days for C. difficile. Uses: (1) serious MRSA infections — bacteraemia, endocarditis, pneumonia (hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated), osteomyelitis, septic arthritis, skin and soft tissue infections, meningitis; (2) infections by MRSE — prosthetic valve endocarditis (with rifampicin and gentamicin), prosthetic joint, vascular graft and catheter-related infections, CSF shunt infections; (3) empirical therapy — bacterial meningitis (with ceftriaxone/cefotaxime, to cover penicillin-resistant pneumococci), febrile neutropenia with suspected line infection, skin infection or haemodynamic instability, sepsis in MRSA-prevalent settings; (4) enterococcal endocarditis in penicillin allergy (with gentamicin); (5) Clostridioides difficile infection — oral vancomycin (first-line along with fidaxomicin; IV ineffective for colitis); (6) surgical prophylaxis in MRSA carriers or β-lactam allergy (cardiac, orthopaedic implant surgery); (7) peritonitis in peritoneal dialysis (intraperitoneal); (8) intraventricular for shunt infections. Adverse effects: (1) infusion-related reactions — 'red man' (red neck/vancomycin flushing) syndrome: pruritus, flushing and erythematous rash of face, neck and upper trunk, sometimes hypotension and angioedema, due to direct non-immune histamine release from mast cells with rapid infusion; prevented by infusing over at least 60 minutes (1 g/h max) and antihistamine premedication; not true allergy; (2) nephrotoxicity — acute interstitial nephritis/tubular injury, related to high troughs and AUC, prolonged therapy, combination with aminoglycosides, piperacillin–tazobactam, amphotericin, loop diuretics, contrast; (3) ototoxicity — tinnitus, hearing loss (rare; high levels, with aminoglycosides); (4) thrombophlebitis (dilute, rotate sites), pain on infusion; (5) hypersensitivity — fever, rashes, eosinophilia, DRESS, linear IgA bullous dermatosis, Stevens–Johnson syndrome; (6) haematological — reversible neutropenia, thrombocytopenia (immune) with prolonged use; (7) anaphylaxis (rare). Alternatives for MRSA: linezolid, daptomycin, teicoplanin, ceftaroline, tigecycline, dalbavancin; for VRE — linezolid, daptomycin.",
[["Key features",[["Feature","Detail"],["Class","Glycopeptide"],["MOA","Binds D-Ala-D-Ala → blocks transglycosylation/transpeptidation"],["Spectrum","Gram-positive only (MRSA, enterococci, C. difficile)"],["Oral","Not absorbed — C. difficile colitis"],["Excretion","Renal (dose by CrCl)"],["Monitoring","AUC/MIC 400–600 (trough 15–20)"]]],
["Uses",["MRSA bacteraemia, endocarditis, pneumonia, osteomyelitis","MRSE prosthetic/line infections","Empirical meningitis (with ceftriaxone)","Febrile neutropenia with line infection","Enterococcal endocarditis (penicillin allergy)","Oral: C. difficile colitis","Surgical prophylaxis (MRSA)"]],
["Adverse effects",[["ADR","Prevention / note"],["Red man syndrome","Infuse ≥ 60 min, antihistamine"],["Nephrotoxicity","Avoid aminoglycosides, monitor levels"],["Ototoxicity","Rare; with other ototoxic drugs"],["Thrombophlebitis","Dilute, rotate site"],["Neutropenia, thrombocytopenia","Prolonged therapy"],["DRESS, rash","Hypersensitivity"]]]],
{i:"Vancomycin remains the reference drug for serious MRSA infections despite slower killing than β-lactams and emerging resistance.",f:"Peptidoglycan precursor (lipid II–NAM-pentapeptide…D-Ala-D-Ala)\n Vancomycin binds D-Ala-D-Ala → blocks transglycosylase + transpeptidase\n → defective wall → lysis (Gram + only)\n VRE: D-Ala-D-Lac → ↓ binding",c:"Vancomycin is the cornerstone for MRSA and other resistant Gram-positive infections and, orally, for C. difficile; slow infusion and drug-level monitoring prevent red man syndrome and nephrotoxicity."});

Z("pharm10",15,"ph",["Urinary tract infections (UTIs) are usually caused by E. coli; uncomplicated cystitis is treated with short courses of nitrofurantoin, fosfomycin or cotrimoxazole, while pyelonephritis and complicated UTIs need fluoroquinolones, cephalosporins or other parenteral agents guided by urine culture.","Lower UTI (bladder) needs a short, local 'urine-concentrated' drug; upper UTI (kidney) is a tissue infection needing drugs that reach good blood levels.",["Uncomplicated cystitis (women): nitrofurantoin 100 mg BD × 5 days, fosfomycin 3 g single dose, cotrimoxazole DS BD × 3 days (if resistance < 20%), pivmecillinam; avoid FQ for cystitis.","Pyelonephritis: outpatient — ciprofloxacin/levofloxacin × 7 days or oral cephalosporin; inpatient — IV ceftriaxone, aminoglycoside, piperacillin–tazobactam, carbapenem (ESBL).","Complicated UTI/catheter/men: culture-guided, 7–14 days; prostatitis — FQ/cotrimoxazole 4–6 weeks; pregnancy — nitrofurantoin (avoid near term), cephalexin, amoxicillin–clavulanate, fosfomycin; treat asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy.","Recurrent UTI prophylaxis: nitrofurantoin/cotrimoxazole low dose at night or postcoital, cranberry, methenamine, vaginal oestrogen (postmenopausal); urinary antiseptics — nitrofurantoin, methenamine, nalidixic acid."],"Cystitis: 'Nitrofurantoin or Fosfomycin'; pyelonephritis: 'FQ or ceftriaxone'."],
"Urinary tract infection is bacterial invasion of the urinary tract — lower (urethritis, cystitis, prostatitis) or upper (pyelonephritis, renal abscess); classified as uncomplicated (non-pregnant women with normal urinary tract) or complicated (men, pregnancy, structural/functional abnormality, catheter, obstruction, stones, diabetes, immunosuppression, renal failure, healthcare-associated). Organisms: Escherichia coli (75–90% of community UTI), Klebsiella, Proteus mirabilis (stones), Staphylococcus saprophyticus (young women), Enterococcus, Pseudomonas, Enterobacter, Serratia, Candida (catheters, hospital); ESBL-producing and carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales increasingly common in India. General principles: urine culture and sensitivity before treatment in complicated, recurrent, upper UTI, pregnancy, men; empirical therapy based on local resistance; adequate hydration; relieve obstruction; remove/change catheters; short courses for cystitis; treat asymptomatic bacteriuria only in pregnancy and before urological procedures. Drugs: (1) urinary antiseptics — nitrofurantoin (bactericidal at urinary concentrations; damages bacterial DNA via reactive intermediates; no systemic levels — not for pyelonephritis; ADR — nausea, brown urine, acute pulmonary hypersensitivity, chronic pulmonary fibrosis, peripheral neuropathy, hepatitis, haemolysis in G6PD deficiency and neonates; avoid when eGFR < 30–45 and at term), methenamine hippurate (releases formaldehyde in acidic urine; prophylaxis), nalidixic acid (historical), fosfomycin trometamol (single 3 g oral dose; inhibits MurA, early cell wall step; active against ESBL E. coli, E. faecalis); (2) cotrimoxazole and trimethoprim; (3) β-lactams — amoxicillin–clavulanate, cephalexin, cefuroxime, cefpodoxime, cefixime (less effective for cystitis), pivmecillinam, ceftriaxone, cefepime, piperacillin–tazobactam, carbapenems (ertapenem, meropenem — ESBL); (4) fluoroquinolones — ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin, norfloxacin (urinary), ofloxacin (good for pyelonephritis and prostatitis; avoid for uncomplicated cystitis due to resistance and toxicity); (5) aminoglycosides — gentamicin, amikacin (single daily dose; concentrated in urine and renal cortex); (6) others — colistin, polymyxin B, ceftazidime–avibactam (CRE), nitrofurantoin for VRE cystitis, fluconazole/amphotericin (Candida). Regimens: (a) acute uncomplicated cystitis in women — nitrofurantoin monohydrate/macrocrystals 100 mg BD × 5 days; or fosfomycin 3 g single dose; or cotrimoxazole DS (160/800 mg) BD × 3 days if local resistance < 20%; or pivmecillinam 400 mg BD × 3–5 days; β-lactams (cephalexin, amoxicillin–clavulanate) × 5–7 days as alternatives; fluoroquinolones reserved; (b) acute uncomplicated pyelonephritis — outpatient: ciprofloxacin 500 mg BD × 7 days or levofloxacin 750 mg OD × 5 days (if resistance < 10%; often preceded by a single IV dose of ceftriaxone or aminoglycoside), oral cotrimoxazole × 14 days if susceptible; inpatient: IV ceftriaxone 1–2 g daily, gentamicin/amikacin, piperacillin–tazobactam, or carbapenem (ertapenem/meropenem if ESBL risk); switch to oral on improvement; total 7–14 days; (c) complicated UTI and catheter-associated UTI — culture-directed; broad initial cover (piperacillin–tazobactam, carbapenem, aminoglycoside); 7 days if prompt response, 10–14 days if delayed; remove/replace catheter; (d) UTI in men — 7–14 days (fluoroquinolone or cotrimoxazole); acute bacterial prostatitis — 2–4 weeks; chronic bacterial prostatitis — fluoroquinolone or cotrimoxazole 4–6 weeks (good prostatic penetration); (e) UTI and asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy — screen and treat (prevents pyelonephritis, preterm labour): nitrofurantoin (avoid in first trimester if possible and at term), cephalexin, amoxicillin (if sensitive), amoxicillin–clavulanate, fosfomycin; avoid fluoroquinolones, tetracyclines, cotrimoxazole in first trimester and near term; pyelonephritis in pregnancy — IV ceftriaxone; (f) children — oral cephalosporins, amoxicillin–clavulanate, cotrimoxazole; IV ceftriaxone/aminoglycoside in infants and febrile UTI; imaging for anomalies (VUR); (g) recurrent UTI (≥ 2 in 6 months or ≥ 3 in 1 year) — behavioural measures (hydration, postcoital voiding), continuous low-dose prophylaxis at bedtime (nitrofurantoin 50–100 mg, cotrimoxazole SS, cephalexin 250 mg, fosfomycin 3 g every 10 days) for 6–12 months or postcoital single dose, vaginal oestrogen in postmenopausal women, methenamine hippurate, cranberry products (modest benefit), self-initiated therapy; (h) fungal UTI — fluconazole. Symptomatic relief: urinary alkalinisers (potassium citrate), phenazopyridine (urinary analgesic; orange urine; short use).",
[["Treatment by type",[["Type","First-line","Duration"],["Uncomplicated cystitis","Nitrofurantoin, fosfomycin, cotrimoxazole, pivmecillinam","5 d / single / 3 d / 3–5 d"],["Pyelonephritis (outpatient)","Ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin; oral cephalosporin","5–14 days"],["Pyelonephritis (inpatient)","Ceftriaxone, aminoglycoside, pip-tazo, carbapenem","7–14 days"],["Complicated / catheter","Culture-guided broad spectrum","7–14 days"],["Prostatitis","Fluoroquinolone, cotrimoxazole","2–6 weeks"],["Pregnancy","Nitrofurantoin, cephalexin, amox-clav, fosfomycin","5–7 days"]]],
["Recurrent UTI prophylaxis",["Hydration, postcoital voiding","Low-dose nitrofurantoin/cotrimoxazole/cephalexin at night","Postcoital single dose","Vaginal oestrogen (postmenopausal)","Methenamine hippurate, cranberry"]],
["Drugs to avoid in pregnancy",["Fluoroquinolones","Tetracyclines","Cotrimoxazole (1st trimester, term)","Nitrofurantoin at term","Aminoglycosides (unless essential)"]]],
{c:"UTI therapy is matched to site and complexity: short courses of urinary-concentrated agents for cystitis, systemic agents for pyelonephritis and complicated infections, culture-guided choices to counter rising resistance, and special care in pregnancy."});
